window.SEARCH = [{"href": "strategy.html", "title": "OCP Java SE 21 溫習策略", "hay": "OCP Java SE 21 溫習策略 考試是 1Z0-830 。50 題，120 分鐘，合格是 68%，即 34／50 。考試日、每週日期、假日同 mock 排期只寫在 OCP-Java-SE-21-study-plan.md 。這一份只講怎樣溫。 你由零開始。時間表的形狀不要改：先用八個星期學完所有課題，再用一個月做 mock，最後三個星期只重複錯題，不要在 mock 高峰之後停下來。詳細到每一天做什麼，看 15 週計劃。 三個階段 階段 時數 做什麼 學完整份 syllabus 168 小時 每個課題學一次。syllabus 結束後，書變成參考，不再逐章重讀 Mock 月 84 小時 先按弱項練，再做計時 mock 1 至 5 保住分數 約 48 小時 Mock 6、有需要才做 mock 7，加上錯題本 考試 — 50 題，120 分鐘 平日是星期一、三、五，每天 3 小時。普通週末每天 6 小時。第 4、7、8 週的週末每天 8 小時。有兩個星期五放假的星期（見 15 週計劃），那兩週各剩 15 小時：周一、周三各 3 小時，周六 6 小時，周日 3 小時。 每天怎麼用這三小時 20 分鐘 ：只溫昨天錯過的東西。Anki 只翻已經學過、而且錯過的卡。 2 小時 ：把當天筆記裡的程式自己打一遍。先寫下你預測的結果，再執行。 40 分鐘 ：做當天的題。每一題錯了，就在錯題本寫一條規則，再寫一個五行的程式。 週末多出來的時間用在做題和錯題本，不用來重讀已經懂的章。第 4、7、8 週的週末是 8 小時。新知識大約到第五、六個小時就開始進不去。如果連續三題都是新規則，就收工。如果只是在重做已經會的題，也收工。 計時的是題目和錯題，不是「我坐了幾小時」。 三份資料怎樣一起用 每一週有三樣東西，分工不同。 檔案 什麼時候用 Week-0N-notes.md 當天。打裡面的程式，做裡面的題 Week-0N-glossary.md 看到一個詞不記得意思時才查。只開本週那一份 Week-0N-anki.apkg 本週日學完、做完題之後才匯入或取消隱藏 Anki 記住的是規則。考試給你的是一段程式，要判斷它不能編譯、執行時才錯，還是印出什麼。這個判斷來自你自己打過的程式，不是來自翻卡。 未到那個星期，不要翻那個星期的卡，也不要讀那個星期的 glossary。還沒寫過 stream，就去背 flatMap ，那些卡會變成噪音。 錯題本 由第一週開始用，mock 月開始之後它就是你的溫習內容。猜對的也算錯。沒有寫進錯題本的覆盤不算完成。 日期： 題目或片段： 我選了什麼： 規則（一句）： 五行程式： 一章如果已經拿到 80%，八週之內不要整章重讀。它會在 mock 月以錯題的形式回來。 每週的門檻 門檻有兩個部分的，兩個都要過。題目的 70% 是向上取整，所以下面的分數就是筆記裡的合格線。 週 合格線 另外要做到的 1 34 題裡 24 題 八種 primitive、15 條運算式、一個含 if／switch／迴圈的程式 2 30 題裡 21 題 另外一個小時關掉自動完成 3 32 題裡 23 題 能在紙上追出父類再子類的初始化順序。M4 要講得出哪一個物件可以被回收 4 30 題裡 21 題 覆寫規則抄成一張卡。巢狀類別各能看見什麼，寫一頁筆記 5 20 題裡 14 題 星期五放假。15 個日期格式先預測再執行 6 20 題裡 14 題 星期五放假 7 30 題裡 21 題 低於 18 題：mock 月的頭幾天只做 stream，先寫程式，再碰其他課題 8 20 題裡 13 題 65%。在錯題本最上面寫下最弱的三個課題。syllabus 在第 8 週日結束 第 1 週未到 24／34，不要硬進第 2 週。後面的繼承、stream、module 都建在頭兩個星期上。用 Helsinki Java MOOC 第一部分補幾天，再回到筆記，而不是帶著還不熟的 var 和 switch 去學 class。 一個程式試兩次還編譯不過，就只留下那一條規則和五行程式，然後繼續。不要把兩個小時花在同一個分號上。 做題時關掉自動完成 筆記只要求第 2 週有一個小時完全關掉自動完成。考試沒有 IntelliJ。 由第 1 週開始：打新程式的那兩個小時可以開著建議，否則你會停在那裡找語法。做題的 40 分鐘關掉。設定在 IntelliJ：Settings → Editor → General → Code Completion，清除 “Show suggestions as you type”。做完題再打開。 書和 mock 八個星期裡只讀一本：Boyarsky 與 Selikoff 的 OCP Oracle Certified Professional Java SE 21 Developer Study Guide （考試編號 1Z0-830）。勘誤在 selikoff.net/ocp21 。筆記裡的題是練習，不是書上的題，也不是真題。 Enthuware 1Z0-830 等到 mock 月開始 才打開（日期見 15 週計劃）。現在做 mock，量到的是你還沒學的課題，不能用來判斷程度。 Mock 的目標（排期見 15 週計劃）： 哪一次 目標 計時 mock 1 65% mock 2 70% mock 3 75% mock 4 75% mock 5 80%（40／50） mock 6 80% mock 7，只有分數還未穩才做 80% 一次 mock 是 120 分鐘，閉卷，不暫停。覆盤沒有寫進錯題本，這一次分數不算。 在 mock 月中段之前訂考試位子。 最後兩次計時 mock 都要達到 75%（38／50） 才去考。合格線只是 68%。75% 是留給考試當天的緊張。未到這條線，不要提前去考。 考試當天怎麼答 大約兩分鐘一題。第一遍先做會的，其餘做記號，然後回來。空白和答錯扣一樣的分，所以每一題都要作答。題目說選兩個，就選兩個。先找不能編譯的那一行，或者會丟出例外的那一行，再去算結果。 考試前一天最多溫兩個小時，核對證件和考場或線上考試的設定，然後停。考試當天去考。"}, {"href": "study-plan.html", "title": "OCP Java SE 21 study plan", "hay": "OCP Java SE 21 study plan Exam 1Z0-830 (Oracle Certified Professional: Java SE 21 Developer) on Sunday 7 March 2027 . You start from no Java on Monday 23 November 2026 . The first eight weeks cover the whole syllabus. The next month is mock exams. The last three weeks stay on mocks so the score is still there on exam morning. Questions 50, multiple choice Time 120 minutes Pass mark 68% = 34 of 50 Sit the exam when The last two timed mocks are both 75% or higher (38 of 50) Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each. Swap the names if your free days differ. A normal weekend day is 6 hours. Weeks 4, 7, and 8 use the full 8 hours each weekend day. Hours Phase Dates Hours What you do Syllabus 23 Nov 2026 – 17 Jan 2027 168 h Every exam topic, once Mock month 18 Jan – 14 Feb 2027 84 h Drills, then timed mocks 1–5 Hold the score 15 Feb – 6 Mar 2027 about 48 h Mocks 6–7 and the notebook Exam Sunday 7 Mar 2027 — 50 questions, 120 minutes About 300 hours in total. Friday 25 December and Friday 1 January are already days off, so those two weeks inside the sprint are 15 hours, not 21. Block Hours Foundations (weeks 1–2) 42 OOP (weeks 3–4) 46 Exceptions and dates (week 5) 15 Collections (week 6) 15 Lambdas and streams (week 7) 25 I/O, modules, threads (week 8) 25 Mock month (weeks 9–12) 84 Last three weeks (weeks 13–15) 48 If a chapter is still open on Sunday 17 January, it becomes the first repair in the mock month. After that Sunday the book is a reference. You study the questions you missed. How a session is split Knowledge week, 3 hours on a weekday 20 min: review yesterday’s misses 2 h: the chapter, every listing typed by you 40 min: questions, then the notebook Mock week, 3 hours on a weekday 20 min: review yesterday’s misses 2 h 20 min: code the traps you missed 20 min: notebook A full mock is 120 minutes. It belongs on a weekend day, or on the Wednesday slots in February. The next session reviews it. On a heavy weekend day the extra two hours go into questions. Predict the output before you run the code. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. A chapter you scored 80% on does not get a second full reading during the sprint. It comes back as questions in January. You work about 80 to 100 book pages in each of the first eight weeks, because the pages are listings and questions. Where each exam topic sits Oracle publishes the topic list and does not publish how many questions each topic gets. Exam topic Week Numeric types, boolean logic, operators 1 Program flow 1 Text, String, StringBuilder, arrays 2 Classes, encapsulation, immutability 3 Inheritance, interfaces, records, sealed types, patterns 4 Exceptions 5 Date, time, duration, period, time zones 5 Collections, sequenced collections, generics 6 Lambdas and streams 7 I/O and NIO.2 8 Localization, plus logging and annotations 8 Modules 8 Concurrency and virtual threads 8 Generics are week 6. Annotations and the logging API are week 8, at recognition level. The published 1Z0-830 topics do not include JDBC. Part 1 — Eight-week syllabus (23 Nov – 17 Jan) Week 1 23–29 Nov 2026 Types, operators, and flow 21 h. Gate: chapter questions at 70%. Mon install JDK 21 and IntelliJ, print one line from main . Wed eight primitives, literals, var . Fri operators, precedence, and casts. Write the answer before you run it. Sat Math , if / else, and both kinds of switch. Sun: loops, break , continue , then the review questions. Install JDK 21 and IntelliJ IDEA Community. java -version prints 21. A class with main prints your name. Write the eight primitives, their sizes, and their default values from memory. Fifteen expressions on precedence, promotion, and casts. Write the answer first, then run. Keep the misses. One program that uses if / else, a switch expression, for , while , break , and continue . Call Math.abs , min , max , round , ceil , floor , and pow . Math.round on a double returns a long. Building-blocks, operators, and decisions review questions at 70%. Each miss: one rule and a five-line program in the notebook. Week 2 30 Nov–6 Dec 2026 Methods, text, and arrays 21 h. Gate: 70%, plus one hour with autocomplete off. Mon methods, return, overloading. Wed varargs and pass-by-value. Fri String , StringBuilder , and a text block. Sat arrays, sort, binarySearch . Sun: autocomplete off for one hour, then questions. Overload one method three ways, including varargs. Varargs is the last parameter, and there is only one. Prove pass-by-value: reassigning the parameter does not change the caller; mutating the object does. One call each for substring , indexOf , equals , strip , isBlank , indent , formatted , and a text block. The end index of substring is exclusive. Show, in a loop, why String concatenation copies and StringBuilder does not. One sentence in the notebook. Reverse an array in place, sort it, and binarySearch it. A negative result encodes the insertion point. Make one jagged two-dimensional array. Autocomplete off for one hour. Methods, text, and arrays questions at 70%. Week 3 7–13 Dec 2026 Classes and memory 21 h. Gate: you can hand-trace initializer order, and score 70%. Mon classes, fields, constructors, this . Wed static members and initializer order. Fri encapsulation. Sat an immutable class and five garbage-collection diagrams. Sun: chapter questions. A class with fields, a constructor, and this . No public fields. Hand-write static-then-instance initializer order for a superclass and a subclass. Run code that prints the order and compare. An encapsulated BankAccount : private fields, constructor, methods. An immutable Money class: private final fields, no setters, class is final. Five memory diagrams. A box is an object, an arrow is a reference. Mark what is eligible for collection. Class-design questions at 70%, including one you can explain about garbage collection. Week 4 14–20 Dec 2026 Inheritance through pattern matching 25 h. Weekend days are 8 h. Gate: mixed OOP questions at 70%. This is the heavy week before the holidays. Mon extends , super , overriding rules on one card. Wed reference type versus object type, and field hiding. Fri equals , hashCode , interfaces, and enums. Sat (8 h) records, sealed types, switch patterns, record patterns. Sun (8 h): nested-class rules in the notebook, then a mixed OOP set. Animal , Dog , and Cat . Overriding rules on one card: signature, covariant return, access, checked exceptions. One example where a field is hidden, not overridden. The reference type decides what compiles; the object type decides which override runs. A correct equals and hashCode , and one broken equals . If equals is true, the hash codes match. An interface with a default method and a static method, two implementers, and an enum with a field and a method. A sealed interface with an exhaustive switch, a record with a compact constructor, a record pattern, and a when guard. A general pattern above a specific one does not compile. Mixed questions on inheritance, interfaces, records, and patterns at 70%. Nested classes: one notebook page on what each kind can see. Week 5 21–27 Dec 2026 Exceptions and the date-time API 15 h. Holiday week. Gate: exceptions and dates at 70%. Friday 25 Dec is off. Mon try / catch / finally , multi-catch, checked versus unchecked. Wed try-with-resources and a custom checked exception. Fri 25 Dec: off. Sat LocalDate , LocalTime , LocalDateTime , Period , Duration , Instant . Sun: one daylight-saving example, 15 formatter predictions, then questions. From memory: checked versus unchecked versus Error , and what finally does when the catch block throws. A custom checked exception, and a try-with-resources class whose close messages print in reverse order of creation. LocalDate , LocalTime , LocalDateTime , Instant , Period , and Duration . Period is date-based. Duration is time-based. plusDays returns a new object. On a real daylight-saving date, show a local time that is skipped or repeated, and what ZonedDateTime does. Predict 15 DateTimeFormatter patterns, then run them. Exceptions and date-time questions at 70%. Week 6 28 Dec 2026–3 Jan 2027 Collections and generics 15 h. Holiday week. Gate: collections questions at 70%. Friday 1 Jan is off. Mon List , and the remove overload trap. Wed Set , Map , null rules, and order. Fri 1 Jan: off. Sat Deque , sequenced methods, Comparable , and Comparator . Sun: generics, then the questions. Show list.remove(1) versus list.remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) . The same data in a List , a HashSet , a TreeSet , and a HashMap . Write down what is ordered and what allows null. Use a Deque as a stack and as a queue. Call getFirst , getLast , addFirst , addLast , and reversed . Sort with Comparable and with Comparator.comparing followed by thenComparing . For ? extends and for ? super , say whether you can add and whether you can get. One example of each. Collections and generics questions at 70%. Week 7 4–10 Jan 2027 Lambdas and streams 25 h. Weekend days are 8 h. Gate: 30 stream questions at 70%. Under 60% means mock week 1 opens with streams. Mon lambda syntax and effectively final variables. Wed Predicate , Function , Consumer , Supplier , UnaryOperator , and method references. Fri Optional , including orElse versus orElseGet . Sat (8 h) pipelines, on paper first. Sun (8 h): groupingBy , primitive streams, 30 questions. Rewrite eight anonymous classes as lambdas, then as method references: static, bound, unbound, and constructor. Name the functional interface from a snippet, 15 out of 15. Include a primitive form such as IntPredicate or ToIntFunction . Optional map , flatMap , orElse , and orElseGet . Show that orElse evaluates its argument even when a value is present. On paper, 12 pipelines using filter , map , flatMap , sorted , distinct , limit , reduce , and collect . Then type them. groupingBy into a map of lists, and into a map of counts. partitioningBy into true and false. IntStream.average returns OptionalDouble . Reuse a consumed stream and read the exception. 30 stream questions at 70%. Under 60%: the first days of the mock month are streams, coded, before any other objective. Week 8 11–17 Jan 2027 I/O, modules, and threads 25 h. Weekend days are 8 h. Gate: mixed quiz at 65%. Sunday 17 Jan the syllabus is closed. Mon byte and character streams, Path , and Files . Wed serialization, locales, resource bundles. Fri modules, compiled in a terminal. Sat (8 h) threads, executors, a race, virtual threads. Sun (8 h): mixed quiz and the weak-topic list. With Path and try-with-resources, read a file, count words, and write a report. One notebook example each for resolve , relativize , and normalize . State what transient and serialVersionUID do. A resource bundle in two locales, printing a date and a currency. Search order is full locale, then language, then the base bundle. Two modules, one exports and one requires , run with java -p and -m . Then a service with provides , with , and uses . Break a missing exports once and save the error. A counter that loses updates, then the same counter fixed with synchronized or AtomicInteger . Run tasks on a fixed pool and on newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor . From memory: thread states, Runnable versus Callable , start versus run , shutdown versus shutdownNow . Calling run does not start a thread. Future.get blocks. Mixed quiz on I/O, locale, modules, and threads at 65%. Write your three weakest topics at the top of the notebook. The book is now a reference, not a reading plan. Part 2 — Mock month (18 Jan – 14 Feb) Five timed mocks. Review is part of the mock: a score with no notebook lines does not count. When What you sit Target 18–24 Jan Objective drills, untimed A ranked weak list 31 Jan, Sunday review Timed mock 1 65% 3 Feb, Wednesday Timed mock 2 70% 6 Feb, Saturday Timed mock 3 75% 10 Feb, Wednesday Timed mock 4 75% 13 Feb, Saturday Timed mock 5 80% (40 of 50) Week 9 18–24 Jan 2027 Find the weak objectives 21 h. Gate: a ranked list of objectives. No full mock yet. Mon 40 questions on OOP and exceptions, review the same day. Wed 40 on collections and streams. Fri 30 on dates, I/O, and locales. Sat 30 on modules and threads, and code every miss. Sun: turn the notebook into a one-page rule sheet, ranked worst objective to best. 40 Enthuware questions on OOP and exceptions. Review each miss before Wednesday. 40 questions on collections and streams. If streams were under 60% in week 7, this day is streams only. 30 questions on dates, I/O, and localization. 30 questions on modules and concurrency. Code every miss, including the ones you guessed. One page: objectives ranked from weakest to strongest, with the rule you keep breaking. No timed mock this week. The rank list is the gate. Week 10 25–31 Jan 2027 Mock 1 21 h. Gate: timed mock 1 at 65% after a full review. Mon and Wed: code the traps from your weakest objective only. Fri 20 mixed questions from that objective. Sat timed mock 1, 120 minutes, closed book, then start the review. Sun: finish the review. A second mock waits until every miss has a notebook line. Two weekday sessions on the single weakest objective. Small programs. Friday: 20 questions on that same objective. Saturday: Enthuware timed mock 1. 50 questions, 120 minutes, no book, no pause. Sunday: every miss becomes a rule plus a five-line program. Guessed answers count as misses. Record the score. 65% is the target. Under 55% means next week repairs two objectives and still takes mock 2. The review is finished before any new mock is opened. Week 11 1–7 Feb 2027 Mocks 2 and 3 21 h. Gate: mock 2 at 70%. Mock 3 at 75%. Mon code the new weakest objective. Wed timed mock 2 (2 hours) and mark it (1 hour). Fri review mock 2. Sat timed mock 3. Sun: review mock 3. Monday: code the traps from whichever objective mock 1 scored lowest. Wednesday: timed mock 2. Target 70%. Friday: review mock 2 completely. Saturday: timed mock 3. Target 75% (38 of 50). Sunday: review mock 3. Update the ranked list. Both reviews are in the notebook. The lower of the two scores decides next Monday’s topic. Week 12 8–14 Feb 2027 Mocks 4 and 5 21 h. Gate: mock 5 at 80% (40 of 50). This Sunday closes the mock month. Mon one objective, coded. Wed timed mock 4. Fri review it. Sat timed mock 5. Sun: review, and write the score you are carrying into the last three weeks. Monday: the objective that is still under 70% across the mocks so far. Wednesday: timed mock 4. Target 75%. Friday: review mock 4. Saturday: timed mock 5. Target 80%, 40 of 50. Sunday: review mock 5. Circle the rules that appeared in more than one mock. Mock month ends. Later weeks repeat mocks and the notebook. Part 3 — Hold the score (15 Feb – 6 Mar) The exam is three weeks after the mock month ends. These weeks repeat mocks and the notebook. When What you sit Target 20 Feb, Saturday Timed mock 6 80% 27 Feb, if needed Timed mock 7 80% Before 7 Mar Last two timed mocks Both 75% or higher Week 13 15–21 Feb 2027 Mock 6 21 h. Gate: mock 6 at 80%, or a written list of what is still under 75%. Mon and Wed: the rules that showed up in more than one mock, coded again. Fri 30 mixed questions at exam pace (about 70 minutes) plus review. Sat timed mock 6. Sun: review. Re-code the repeated traps from the notebook. Two weekday sessions. Friday: 30 mixed questions at two minutes each, then review. Saturday: timed mock 6. Target 80%. Sunday: review mock 6 the same day. A miss with no notebook line is unfinished. Practice the exam habit: first pass for what you know, flag the rest, answer every question. If mock 6 is under 75%, next week is another full mock. If it is 80% or higher, next week is the notebook. Week 14 22–28 Feb 2027 Mock 7 only if the score moved 15 h if mock 6 was 80% or higher, otherwise 21 h. Gate: two recent mocks at 75% or higher. If mock 6 was under 75%: Mon and Wed repair, Sat timed mock 7, Sun review. If mock 6 was 80% or higher: Mon, Wed, and Sat are notebook and flashcards only, about 3 hours each. Friday stays light either way. Choose from mock 6: another timed mock, or notebook only. Write the choice down before Monday. Two sessions on the objective that still costs the most points. Timed mock 7 if the last mock was under 80%. Target 80%. Skip it if you are already there twice. Review that mock the next day, or spend the same hours rereading the one-page rule sheet. The last two timed mocks are both 75% or higher (38 of 50). That is the line for sitting on 7 Mar. The rule sheet is one page. If it has grown, cut it back to the rules you still miss. Week 15 1–7 Mar 2027 Taper, then the exam About 12 h, then stop. Saturday is two hours. Sunday 7 Mar is the exam. Mon a timed mock only if you are still under 75%. Otherwise flashcards for 3 hours. Wed rule sheet, 3 hours. Fri flashcards, stop by 18:00. Sat two hours in the morning, confirm ID and the test center or the online setup, then stop. Sun: exam. Monday: timed mock 8 only if the last two mocks are not both at 75%. Otherwise the notebook. Wednesday: one-page rule sheet, out loud. Friday: three hours of flashcards. No mock after 18:00. Saturday: at most two hours. Confirm the start time, the ID, and the test center or OnVUE setup. Exam pace: about two minutes a question, flag and move, answer every question. Sunday 7 Mar: 1Z0-830. 50 questions, 120 minutes, pass at 34. Books and tools Material Role Jeanne Boyarsky and Scott Selikoff, OCP Oracle Certified Professional Java SE 21 Developer Study Guide , exam 1Z0-830 The only book during the 8 weeks. One pass. Errata: selikoff.net/ocp21 Enthuware 1Z0-830 Objective drills from 18 Jan, then the timed mocks. This is the score you trust. JDK 21 and IntelliJ IDEA Community Daily coding. Turn autocomplete off for the hour named in week 2. Oracle exam page for 1Z0-830 Topic list. Read it again in the first mock week. Schedule the seat by mid-January. Exam morning 50 questions, 120 minutes, pass at 34. About two minutes each. First pass for the ones you can answer, flag the rest, then return. A wrong answer costs the same as a blank one, so every question gets an answer. If it says choose two, choose two. Look for the line that does not compile, or the line that throws, before you compute a result. Mistake notebook One entry per miss: the rule in a sentence, then a five-line program. From 18 January this is what you study. Date: Question / snippet: What I answered: The rule: Five-line program:"}, {"href": "Week-01-notes.html", "title": "Week 1 notes — types, operators, and flow", "hay": "Week 1 notes — types, operators, and flow 21 hours. This file follows Week 1 of the study plan. Calendar dates are only in the 15-week plan . The gate is the building-blocks, operators, and decisions questions at 70% (24 of the 34 questions in this file). 參考書係 Boyarsky 同 Selikoff 嘅 OCP Oracle Certified Professional Java SE 21 Developer Study Guide 。每章下面嘅頁碼係紙本書。 必睇 先至翻開。 略讀 只核對標題同筆記未寫過嘅一句。 唔使睇 留到標明嗰一週，或者唔使做。每章末嘅 Review Questions 都唔使做，計分用呢份筆記嘅題。書前面嘅 Assessment Test 同網上題庫留到 mock 月開始（見 15 週計劃）。勘誤在 selikoff.net/ocp21 。 Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Saturday and Sunday are 6 hours each. Monday has nothing to review yet, so those 20 minutes are the JDK check. These are the two exam objectives the plan puts in this week: Numeric types and boolean logic: primitives, wrapper classes, precedence, promotion, casts, and Math . Program flow: if / else , switch statements and expressions, loops, break , and continue . String methods, arrays, and methods you write yourself are Week 2. Leave them until then. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon Install JDK 21 and IntelliJ. Print one line from main . Monday lab, then Chapter 1 question Q7 Wed Eight primitives, literals, and var . Primitives through wrappers, then the rest of the Chapter 1 questions Fri Operators, precedence, and casts. Write the answer before you run it. Chapter 2 through the operator traps, then the fifteen expressions, then Chapter 2 questions Q1–Q6, Q9, and Q10 Sat Math , if / else , and both kinds of switch . Math lab, Chapter 2 questions Q7 and Q8, then if and both switches Sun Loops, break , continue , then the review questions. Loops, the one program, Chapter 3 loop questions, the mixed set, then score all 34 Same checklist as the study plan: Install JDK 21 and IntelliJ IDEA Community. java -version prints 21. A class with main prints your name. Write the eight primitives, their sizes, and their default values from memory. Fifteen expressions on precedence, promotion, and casts. Write the answer first, then run. Keep the misses. One program that uses if / else , a switch expression, for , while , break , and continue . Call Math.abs , min , max , round , ceil , floor , and pow . Math.round on a double returns a long . Building-blocks, operators, and decisions review questions at 70%. Each miss: one rule and a five-line program in the notebook. JDK 21 is already installed on this PC at C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-21 (version 21.0.9). JAVA_HOME points there. Monday still confirms java -version , then IntelliJ. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — Building blocks 必睇 參考書第 1 章 Building Blocks，pp. 2–30 同 pp. 34–52：由 “Learning About the Environment” 到 “Managing Variable Scope”，再加 Summary 同 Exam Essentials。 唔使睇 “Defining Text Blocks”（pp. 31–33，第 2 週）、“Destroying Objects”（第 3 週，就在本章 Summary 前面）、Review Questions（p. 53 起）。 What you must know A Java file holds one or more classes. The file name matches the public class. javac Hello.java compiles it to Hello.class . java Hello runs it. The exam uses snippets, so you have to imagine that wrapping class yourself. A valid main The program starts at: public static void main(String[] args) {} These also work: static public void main(String[] args) {} public static void main(String... args) {} public static void main(String args[]) {} public and static may be swapped. The parameter may be a String array or String varargs. The name is main , the return type is void . Anything else ( Main , a return of int , a parameter of int[] ) compiles as an ordinary method and is not the entry point. On Java 21 the exam still uses this classic main . Monday lab — JDK, IntelliJ, one line from main Open a new terminal and run java -version . The first line should contain 21 . PowerShell often shows that line in red because Java writes it to the error stream. The version text is the result. Install IntelliJ IDEA Community if it is not installed yet. In the new project dialog, set the JDK to 21 and the folder C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-21 . Type this class. The printed line is your name. public class Hello { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(\"Your Name\"); } } Run it from IntelliJ. Then answer Chapter 1 question Q7. That is Monday’s question time. Wednesday — eight primitives, literals, and var Close this file and write the eight primitives, their sizes, and their default field values from memory. Then check the table. After the table, read literals and var , and type every listing. Wrappers are the same day’s reading: the week’s exam topic is numeric types, and the Chapter 1 questions use them. End Wednesday with Chapter 1 questions Q1–Q6 and Q8 (40 minutes). Each miss goes in the notebook. The eight primitives Type Size Default on a field Range to remember byte 8-bit 0 -128 to 127 short 16-bit 0 -32,768 to 32,767 int 32-bit 0 about ±2.1 billion long 64-bit 0 needs an L suffix on big literals float 32-bit 0.0f literal needs f double 64-bit 0.0 default for 1.0 char 16-bit unsigned '\\u0000' 0 to 65,535 boolean — false only true or false Fields (instance or static ) get those defaults. Local variables do not. Reading a local variable before it has been assigned is a compile error. public class Defaults { static int count; // 0 static boolean ready; // false static String name; // null, a reference, not a primitive public static void main(String[] args) { int local; // System.out.println(local); // does not compile local = 1; System.out.println(local); // 1 } } String is a class. Its default on a field is null . A local String still must be assigned before you read it. Literals int n = 1_000_000; // underscores sit between digits long big = 3_000_000_000L; float f = 1.0f; double d = 1.0; // 1.0 is a double char c = 'A'; char c2 = 65; // 65 fits in a char, so this compiles boolean b = true; int hex = 0x1F; // 31 int bin = 0b1010; // 10 int oct = 011; // octal: 9, not 11 Rules the exam uses: 1.0 is a double . float f = 1.0; does not compile. float f = 1; does, because an int widens to float . A whole number is an int . long x = 3000000000; does not compile. Write 3000000000L . byte b = 128; does not compile. 128 does not fit. byte b = 127; does. char c = -1; does not compile. A char cannot be negative. char c = (char) -1; compiles. A leading zero means octal. 011 is 9. 018 does not compile, because 8 is not an octal digit. Underscores must sit between digits. _1 , 1_ , 1_.0 , and 1_L do not compile. 1_000 and 1__000 do. var is for local variables only var count = 1; // int var price = 1.0; // double var name = \"ocp\"; // String var nums = new int[] {1, 2}; // int[] These do not compile: var x; // no initializer, so no type to infer var y = null; // null has no type var z = {1, 2}; // an array initializer needs a type on the left, or new var a = 1, b = 2; // only one variable var cannot be a field, a parameter, or a return type. var is a type name, so int var = 1; is legal. var n = (String) null; compiles, because the cast tells the compiler the type. Scope A local variable lives from its declaration to the end of the block. A nested block cannot declare the same name again. public static void main(String[] args) { int x = 1; { // int x = 2; // does not compile: x is already in scope int y = 2; } // y is gone here int y = 3; // legal, the previous y is out of scope } Wrapper classes Each primitive has a class: Byte , Short , Integer , Long , Float , Double , Character , Boolean . Assigning a primitive to a wrapper is boxing . The other direction is unboxing . Integer count = 10; // boxing int n = count; // unboxing On Java 21 the constructors are gone. new Integer(1) does not compile. Use assignment or Integer.valueOf(1) . Integer.parseInt(\"10\") returns an int and throws NumberFormatException on \"ten\" . Integer.valueOf(\"10\") returns an Integer . Unboxing null throws NullPointerException : Integer empty = null; int n = empty; // NullPointerException Integer.valueOf caches -128 to 127 . == on two wrappers compares references. Inside the cache they are often the same object. Outside it, two equal values can be different objects. Use equals to compare values. Integer a = 127; Integer b = 127; System.out.println(a == b); // true, cached Integer c = 128; Integer d = 128; System.out.println(c == d); // false, different objects System.out.println(c.equals(d)); // true Integer e = 1000; int f = 1000; System.out.println(e == f); // true, the wrapper is unboxed Byte , Short , and Long use the same -128 to 127 cache. Character caches 0 to 127. Float and Double do not cache, so == on two Float objects is a reference check even for small values. Chapter 1 questions Q1. What is the output? public class Q1 { static int count; static boolean ready; public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(count); System.out.print(ready); } } A 0false B 0true C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q2. Which of the following compile? (Choose all that apply.) byte a = 127; byte b = 128; long c = 3_000_000_000; long d = 3_000_000_000L; float e = 1.0; char f = 65; int g = 018; int h = 011; A a B b C c D d E e F f G g H h 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the result? public class Q3 { public static void main(String[] args) { int local; System.out.println(local); } } A 0 B null C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q4. Which lines compile? (Choose all that apply.) var a = 10; var b = null; var c = (String) null; var d = {1, 2}; var e = new int[] {1, 2}; var f = 1, g = 2; A The line with a B The line with b C The line with c D The line with d E The line with e F The line with f and g 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the output? public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { Integer a = 128; Integer b = 128; Integer c = 100; Integer d = 100; System.out.print(a == b); System.out.print(\" \"); System.out.print(c == d); System.out.print(\" \"); System.out.print(a.equals(b)); } } A true true true B false true true C false false true D true false true 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the result? public class Q6 { public static void main(String[] args) { Integer n = null; int value = n; System.out.println(value); } } A 0 B null C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the output of this legal entry-point class? If it does not run, choose the reason. public class Q7 { static public void main(String args[]) { System.out.println(\"go\"); } } A go B The code does not compile C It compiles, but there is no valid main , so it does not start D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q8. Which snippet compiles? A { int x = 1; } int x = 2; B int x = 1; { int x = 2; } C int value = 1_000_; D Integer n = new Integer(5); 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Operators 必睇 參考書第 2 章 Operators，pp. 64–92：由 “Understanding Java Operators” 到 Exam Essentials。同第 4 章 “Calculating with Math APIs” pp. 202–203（ min 、 max 、 round 、 ceil 、 floor 、 pow ）。 唔使睇 bitwise operators（p. 86。筆記 “Shifts” 一段就夠）、 BigInteger （p. 204）、第 4 章其餘部分、Review Questions（p. 93 起）。 Friday is this chapter through the operator traps, then the fifteen expressions. Skip Math until Saturday. The shift section is part of Friday only after the fifteen expressions are done; Chapter 2 question Q10 uses one shift. Saturday part 1 is the Math section, the Math lab, and Chapter 2 questions Q7 and Q8. What you must know The exam asks you to evaluate an expression by hand. Precedence decides what happens first. When two operators share a precedence, most of them associate left to right. Assignment and the ternary operator associate right to left. Precedence, highest first Postfix x++ x-- Prefix ++x --x + - ! Cast (int) * / % + - (and string concatenation, which is the same + ) Shifts << >> >>> Relational < > <= >= instanceof Equality == != & ^ | && || Ternary ?: Assignment = += -= *= and the rest instanceof returns in week 4 with classes. For this week, remember that it sits with the relational operators. Numeric promotion Smaller integer types are promoted to int before arithmetic. byte , short , and char arithmetic produces an int . byte a = 1; byte b = 2; byte c = a + b; // does not compile: a + b is an int byte d = 1 + 2; // compiles: 1 + 2 is a constant that fits in a byte Binary promotion for an operator with two numbers: if either side is double , the other becomes double else if either is float , the other becomes float else if either is long , the other becomes long else both become int A widening assignment is free: int to long , float to double . A narrowing assignment needs a cast: long to int , double to int . The cast truncates toward zero. (int) 9.9 is 9 . (int) -9.9 is -9 . A boolean cannot be cast to a number, and a number cannot be cast to a boolean . The operators that generate traps Integer division truncates toward zero. 5 / 2 is 2 . 5 % 2 is 1 . The sign of % follows the dividend: -5 % 2 is -1 . Dividing an integer by zero throws ArithmeticException . Dividing a floating-point value by zero does not: 1.0 / 0.0 is Infinity , and 0.0 / 0.0 is NaN . + is left to right. Once a String appears, the rest is concatenation. System.out.println(1 + 2 + \"3\" + 4 + 5); // 3345 System.out.println(\"3\" + 1 + 2); // 312 && and || stop early. & and | on booleans always evaluate both sides. On numbers, & | ^ are bitwise. int x = 0; boolean left = false && ++x > 0; // x stays 0 boolean both = false & ++x > 0; // x becomes 1 ^ on booleans is exclusive or: true ^ false is true , true ^ true is false . A compound assignment includes a hidden cast, and the right-hand side is evaluated as a whole: byte b = 1; b += 1; // compiles: b = (byte) (b + 1) b = b + 1; // does not compile int n = 5; n *= 2 + 3; // n = 5 * (2 + 3) = 25 x++ returns the old value, then adds one. ++x adds one, then returns the new value. int x = 1; int y = x++ + ++x; // 1 + 3, and x is now 3 The ternary operator associates to the right. Both branches must end up as one type. flag ? 1 : 2.0 has type double . Assigning that to an int does not compile. An assignment is an expression. if (ready = true) assigns true and then tests it, when ready is a boolean . if (n = 5) does not compile when n is an int , because the value of the assignment is an int , and if requires a boolean . Shifts Read this on Friday after the fifteen expressions. << shifts left. >> shifts right and keeps the sign. >>> shifts right and fills with zeros. For an int , the distance is taken mod 32. 1 << 32 is 1 , because the distance is 0. 1 << 31 is Integer.MIN_VALUE . Friday task — fifteen expressions Write the value, and the type when it matters, before you compile anything. Then type each line into main and run it. Keep every miss. 1 + 2 * 3 (1 + 2) * 3 5 / 2 5 / 2.0 (int) (5 / 2.0) (int) 5 / 2.0 (double) (5 / 2) (double) 5 / 2 10 % 3 -5 % 2 (int) 9.9 (int) -9.9 1 + 2 + \"3\" + 4 true || false && false (byte) 200 The answer key is after the Chapter 2 answers. Do not scroll to it yet. Friday’s question time, after the expressions: Chapter 2 questions Q1–Q6, Q9, and Q10. Saturday part 1 — Math Call each method below by typing the lab. Math.round on a double returns a long . That is the checklist item. Call Returns Rule Math.abs same type as the argument Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) stays negative, because the positive value does not fit in an int Math.min / Math.max the type of the overload you called Math.round(double) long ties go toward positive infinity Math.round(float) int same tie rule Math.ceil double toward positive infinity Math.floor double toward negative infinity Math.pow double Math.random double from 0.0 inclusive to 1.0 exclusive Math.round(2.5) is 3 . Math.round(-1.5) is -1 , because the tie rounds toward positive infinity. Math.ceil(-1.1) is -1.0 . Math.floor(-1.1) is -2.0 . int i = Math.round(2.5); does not compile, because that overload returns a long . int i = Math.round(2.5f); compiles. Type this class and run it. Leave the commented line commented, then uncomment it once and confirm the compile error. public class MathCalls { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(Math.abs(-7)); System.out.println(Math.min(3, 8)); System.out.println(Math.max(3, 8)); long rounded = Math.round(2.5); System.out.println(rounded); System.out.println(Math.ceil(1.2)); System.out.println(Math.floor(1.2)); System.out.println(Math.pow(2, 3)); // int bad = Math.round(2.5); } } Saturday’s question time for this half: Chapter 2 questions Q7 and Q8. Then go to Chapter 3 for if / else and both kinds of switch . Chapter 2 questions Q1–Q6, Q9, and Q10 are Friday. Q7 and Q8 are Saturday. Q1. What is the output? public class Q1 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(1 + 2 + \"3\" + 4 + 5); } } A 15 B 3345 C 12345 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. Which lines compile? (Choose all that apply.) byte a = 1; byte b = 2; byte c = a + b; byte d = 1 + 2; byte e = (byte) (a + b); int f = a + b; A The declaration of a B The declaration of c C The declaration of d D The declaration of e E The declaration of f 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the output? public class Q3 { public static void main(String[] args) { int x = 1; int y = x++ + ++x; System.out.print(x); System.out.print(y); } } A 23 B 33 C 34 D 24 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the output? public class Q4 { public static void main(String[] args) { int x = 0; if (false && ++x == 1) {} System.out.print(x); if (false & ++x == 1) {} System.out.print(x); } } A 00 B 01 C 11 D An ArithmeticException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the result? public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { byte b = 1; b += 1; System.out.println(b); } } A 1 B 2 C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the output? public class Q6 { public static void main(String[] args) { int n = 5; n *= 2 + 3; System.out.println(n); } } A 13 B 25 C 10 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the output? public class Q7 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(Math.round(2.5)); System.out.print(\" \"); System.out.print(Math.round(-1.5)); System.out.print(\" \"); System.out.print(Math.floor(-1.1)); } } A 2 -1 -1.0 B 3 -1 -2.0 C 3 -2 -1.0 D 2 -2 -2.0 揭曉答案 Q8. Which of the following compile? (Choose all that apply.) int a = Math.round(2.5); int b = Math.round(2.5f); long c = Math.round(2.5); int d = (int) Math.ceil(1.2); int e = Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE); boolean f = (boolean) 1; A a B b C c D d E The line that calls Math.abs compiles, and the value is negative F f 揭曉答案 Q9. What is the output? public class Q9 { public static void main(String[] args) { int x = 10; int y = 20; System.out.println(x > 5 ? y > 15 ? 1 : 2 : 3); } } A 1 B 2 C 3 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q10. What is the result? public class Q10 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(1 << 32); } } A 0 B 1 C 32 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Friday expression answers 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Making decisions 必睇 參考書第 3 章 Making Decisions：pp. 102–105（ if / else ）、pp. 111–123（ switch 語句同表達式）、pp. 130–149（迴圈、 break 、 continue 、Summary、Exam Essentials）。 唔使睇 “Shortening Code with Pattern Matching”（pp. 106–110）同 “Using Pattern Matching with switch”（pp. 124–129）。呢兩節係第 4 週。Review Questions（p. 150 起）唔使做。 Saturday part 2 is if / else , the switch statement, and the switch expression. Stop before “Loops”. Sunday is loops, break , continue , the one program, and then every review question in this file. What you must know if requires a boolean . An int is not a condition. Braces are optional around one statement, and a declaration cannot be that one statement. if (true) int x = 1; // does not compile else binds to the nearest if . switch statement A classic switch accepts byte , short , char , int , the matching wrappers, String , and (from week 4) an enum. It does not accept long , float , double , or boolean . A case label is a compile-time constant. Duplicate labels do not compile. Without break , execution falls into the next case. default may sit anywhere. A switch statement with no matching case and no default does nothing. int n = 1; switch (n) { case 1: System.out.print(\"A\"); case 2: System.out.print(\"B\"); break; default: System.out.print(\"C\"); } // AB Arrow form is also a statement. Arrows do not fall through, so break is not used: switch (n) { case 1 -> System.out.print(\"A\"); case 2, 3 -> System.out.print(\"B\"); default -> System.out.print(\"C\"); } You cannot mix -> and : in one switch . A null selector throws NullPointerException before any case runs. switch expression A switch expression produces a value. It must be exhaustive, so an int or a String needs a default (or enough cases for the compiler to see that every value is covered). It ends with a semicolon when you assign it. int result = switch (n) { case 1 -> 10; case 2 -> 20; default -> 0; }; A block on the right of -> uses yield to produce the value: int result = switch (n) { case 1 -> { System.out.print(\"A\"); yield 10; } default -> 0; }; Colon form inside an expression uses yield as well. Falling out of a case without yield does not compile. yield belongs to a switch expression. break 10; is not Java. When every constant of an enum is listed, a switch expression on that enum can omit default . Enums are week 4. Pattern matching in switch is week 4 as well. Sunday — loops, break , continue , then the review while tests first. The body can be skipped. do / while runs the body once, then tests. The semicolon after while (...) is required. int i = 0; do { System.out.print(i); } while (i++ < 2); // prints 012 for has init, condition, and update. The init variable’s scope is the loop. All three parts are optional. for (;;) is an infinite loop. The enhanced for walks an array or an Iterable . The loop variable is a new variable each time. Assigning to it does not change the array. A null array throws NullPointerException . int[] nums = {1, 2, 3}; for (int n : nums) { n = 0; // changes the copy, not the array } A declaration cannot be the single statement of a loop: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) int x = i; // does not compile Unreachable code while (false) does not compile. if (false) { ... } does compile, and the lines after the if still run. This special case is only for if . An infinite while (true) makes the lines after the loop unreachable, unless a break makes them reachable. break and continue break leaves the nearest loop or switch . continue starts the next iteration of the nearest loop. A label sits in front of a statement: outer: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) { if (j == 1) break outer; } } break with a label must be inside that labeled loop or switch . continue with a label must be inside that labeled loop. The label name is an identifier, not a String . Sunday task — the one program Type this after the loop section. It is the checklist program: if / else , a switch expression, for , while , break , and continue . Write the output on paper, then run it. public class Week1Flow { public static void main(String[] args) { int n = 2; if (n > 0) { System.out.println(\"positive\"); } else { System.out.println(\"not positive\"); } String label = switch (n) { case 1 -> \"one\"; case 2 -> \"two\"; default -> \"other\"; }; System.out.println(label); for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { if (i == 1) { continue; } System.out.println(\"for \" + i); } int j = 0; while (j < 3) { if (j == 2) { break; } System.out.println(\"while \" + j); j++; } } } The run prints: positive two for 0 for 2 while 0 while 1 Sunday’s question time is Chapter 3 questions Q6–Q10, then the mixed set. Q1–Q5 are the Saturday if and switch questions; do them on Saturday if you have not already. After that, score all 34 questions. 24 or more meets the gate. Every miss becomes one rule and a five-line program before Monday of Week 2. Chapter 3 questions Q1–Q5 are Saturday. Q6–Q10 are Sunday. Q1. What is the output? public class Q1 { public static void main(String[] args) { if (false) if (true) System.out.print(\"A\"); else System.out.print(\"B\"); System.out.print(\"C\"); } } A AC B BC C C D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? public class Q2 { public static void main(String[] args) { int n = 1; switch (n) { case 1: System.out.print(\"A\"); case 2: System.out.print(\"B\"); break; default: System.out.print(\"C\"); } } } A A B AB C ABC D AC 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the result? public class Q3 { public static void main(String[] args) { int n = 2; int result = switch (n) { case 1 -> 10; case 2 -> 20; }; System.out.println(result); } } A 20 B 0 C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the output? public class Q4 { public static void main(String[] args) { int n = 1; int result = switch (n) { case 1 -> { System.out.print(\"A\"); yield 10; } case 2 -> 20; default -> 0; }; System.out.print(result); } } A A10 B 10 C A D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the output? public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { String s = null; switch (s) { default -> System.out.print(\"X\"); } } } A X B No output C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the output? public class Q6 { public static void main(String[] args) { int i = 0; do { System.out.print(i); } while (i++ < 2); } } A 01 B 012 C 0123 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the output? public class Q7 { public static void main(String[] args) { int count = 0; outer: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) { count++; if (i == 1) break outer; } } System.out.println(count); } } A 3 B 4 C 6 D 9 揭曉答案 Q8. Which snippets compile? (Choose all that apply.) A while (false) { System.out.print(\"A\"); } B if (false) { System.out.print(\"A\"); } System.out.print(\"B\"); C for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) int x = i; D int n = 1; switch (n) { case 1 -> System.out.print(\"A\"); case 1 -> System.out.print(\"B\"); } 揭曉答案 Q9. What is the output? public class Q9 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = {1, 2, 3}; for (int n : nums) { n = 9; } System.out.print(nums[0]); } } A 9 B 1 C 123 D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q10. What is the result? public class Q10 { public static void main(String[] args) { boolean ready = false; if (ready = true) System.out.print(\"yes\"); else System.out.print(\"no\"); } } A yes B no C The code does not compile D No output 揭曉答案 Sunday mixed set 唔使睇 呢個混合題用筆記計分。唔好翻第 3 章嘅 Review Questions。 Do this set after Week1Flow and Chapter 3 questions Q6–Q10. Then score Chapter 1 (8), Chapter 2 (10), Chapter 3 (10), and this set (6) together. There are 34 questions. 24 or more is the Week 1 gate from the study plan. Redo every miss before Monday of Week 2. M1. What is the output? public class M1 { static char letter; public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println((int) letter); } } A A blank line B 0 C null D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M2. What is the output? public class M2 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(5 / 2); System.out.print(\" \"); System.out.print(-5 % 2); } } A 2.5 -1 B 2 -1 C 2 1 D An ArithmeticException is thrown 揭曉答案 M3. What is the result? public class M3 { public static void main(String[] args) { int n = 0; switch (n) { case 1 -> System.out.print(\"A\"); default -> System.out.print(\"B\"); } } } A A B B C AB D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M4. What is the output? public class M4 { public static void main(String[] args) { int x = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { if (i == 1) continue; x++; } System.out.println(x); } } A 1 B 2 C 3 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M5. Which line does not compile? int a = 1_000; double b = 1_000.00; float c = 10.0_0F; long d = 10_L; int e = 0x_11; A a B b C c D d E e F More than one of these 揭曉答案 M6. What is the output? public class M6 { public static void main(String[] args) { Integer a = 200; int b = 200; System.out.print(a == b); System.out.print(Math.ceil(1.0)); } } A true1.0 B false1.0 C true2.0 D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card Fields get defaults. Local variables must be assigned before they are read. byte , short , and char arithmetic produces an int . A constant that fits may still be assigned. += casts back to the left-hand type. b = b + 1 does not. && and || can skip the right side. & and | do not. Integer == is a reference compare, except when one side is a primitive and gets unboxed. The cache is -128 to 127. equals compares values. Math.round(double) returns a long . Ties go toward positive infinity, so round(-1.5) is -1 . A switch statement can fall through when it uses colons. A switch expression must cover every value and uses yield inside a block. else binds to the nearest if . while (false) does not compile. if (false) does. break leaves a loop or a switch . continue goes to the next iteration. A label is how you name the outer one."}, {"href": "Week-01-glossary.html", "title": "Week 1 glossary — types, operators, and flow", "hay": "Week 1 glossary — types, operators, and flow Terms from Week-01-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — a program Term Meaning main The entry point. public static void main(String[] args) . public and static may be swapped. The parameter may be String[] or String... . Compile javac Hello.java checks the source and writes Hello.class . A file that fails to compile never runs. Local variable A variable declared inside a method. It has no default. Reading it before it is assigned does not compile. Wednesday — primitives, literals, var , wrappers Term Meaning Primitive One of eight types: byte 8-bit, short 16, int 32, long 64, float 32, double 64, char 16-bit unsigned, boolean . byte range −128 to 127. byte b = 128 does not compile. char An unsigned number from 0 to 65,535. A negative literal such as char c = -1 does not compile. A cast such as (char) -1 does. Field default A field that is not assigned starts as 0 , false , '\\u0000' , or null . Locals do not get these defaults. Literal A value written in source. A whole number is an int unless it ends in L . 1.0 is a double . A float literal ends in f . Octal literal A leading 0 means base 8. 011 is 9. 018 does not compile, because 8 is not an octal digit. Underscore in a number Allowed only between digits. 1_000 compiles. 10_L , 0x_11 , and 1_000_ do not. var One local variable with an initializer the compiler can type. Not a field, a parameter, or a return type. var x; and var y = null do not compile. var n = (String) null does, because the cast supplies the type. Scope The block where a name can be used. An inner block cannot declare int x when the outer block already has int x . After the inner block ends, the name may be declared again. Wrapper An object for a primitive: Integer , Long , Double , and the others. On Java 21, new Integer(1) does not compile. Use Integer n = 1 or Integer.valueOf(1) . Boxing Wrapping a primitive in its wrapper. Integer n = 1 boxes. Unboxing Taking the primitive out of a wrapper. int value = n unboxes. Unboxing null throws NullPointerException . Integer cache Integer reuses one object for each value from −128 to 127. == on two wrappers compares references, so 127 == 127 is true and 128 == 128 is false. equals compares the number. Byte , Short , and Long use the same cache. Character caches 0 to 127. Float and Double do not cache. Wrapper compared with a primitive Integer == int unboxes the wrapper, so the cache does not matter. Integer a = 200; int b = 200; a == b is true. Friday — operators Term Meaning Precedence Which operator runs first. Postfix ++ , then * , then + , then && , then \\|\\| , then ?: . ?: and assignment associate right to left. Most others associate left to right. Numeric promotion byte , short , and char arithmetic produces an int . A constant expression whose result fits may still be assigned: byte d = 1 + 2 compiles, byte c = a + b does not. Compound assignment += , -= , *= , and the rest. The operation includes a hidden cast back to the left-hand type, so b += 1 compiles when b = b + 1 does not. n *= 2 + 3 means n = n * (2 + 3) . Postfix and prefix x++ contributes the current value, then adds 1. ++x adds 1, then contributes the new value. After int x = 1; int y = x++ + ++x , x is 3 and y is 4. Short-circuit && and \\|\\| skip the right side when the result is already known. & and \\| on a boolean always evaluate both sides. Integer division 5 / 2 is 2 . Truncation is toward zero. The sign of % follows the dividend, so -5 % 2 is -1 . An int divided by zero throws ArithmeticException . 1.0 / 0.0 is Infinity . Cast An explicit conversion, such as (int) 5.0 . Casting a double toward zero drops the fraction: (int) 9.9 is 9 and (int) -9.9 is -9 . (boolean) 1 does not compile. A boolean cannot be cast to or from a number. Shift An int shift distance is taken mod 32, so 1 << 32 is 1 << 0 , which is 1 . 1 << 31 is Integer.MIN_VALUE . Ternary condition ? a : b . It associates to the right. Both branches share one type. flag ? 1 : 2.0 has type double . Assignment in if if (ready = true) assigns, then tests the assigned boolean. if (n = 5) does not compile when n is an int , because if needs a boolean. Saturday and Sunday — Math and flow Term Meaning Math.round A tie rounds toward positive infinity. Math.round(2.5) is 3 . Math.round(-1.5) is -1 . round(double) returns long . round(float) returns int . Math.floor Toward negative infinity. floor(-1.1) is -2.0 . Returns double . Math.ceil Toward positive infinity. ceil(-1.1) is -1.0 . Returns double . Math.abs The magnitude. Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) stays negative, because the positive value does not fit in an int . The same trap exists for Long.MIN_VALUE . Dangling else An else belongs to the nearest if . Unreachable while (false) does not compile. if (false) does compile, and the lines after the if still run. Single statement A local-variable declaration cannot be the only statement of an if or a loop. Use a block. Classic switch The selector may be byte , short , char , int , their wrappers, String , or an enum. Not long , float , double , or boolean . A case label is a compile-time constant. Duplicate labels do not compile. Fall-through In a colon switch , a case without break continues into the next case. default may sit anywhere. No match and no default means a switch statement does nothing. Arrow switch Cases use -> and do not fall through. They do not use break . Mixing -> and : in one switch does not compile. Switch expression Produces a value. On an int it must cover every remaining value, so it needs a default or enough cases. A block produces its value with yield . break 10 is not Java. null selector A switch on a null String throws NullPointerException before any case or default runs. do / while The body runs at least once. The semicolon after while (...) is required. int i = 0; do { print i; } while (i++ < 2); prints 012 . break Leaves the nearest loop or switch. break with a label leaves the named enclosing loop or switch. continue Starts the next iteration of the nearest loop. continue with a label must name a loop. Enhanced for for (int n : nums) gives n a separate copy. Assigning to n does not write into the array. A null array throws NullPointerException ."}, {"href": "Week-02-notes.html", "title": "Week 2 notes — methods, text, and arrays", "hay": "Week 2 notes — methods, text, and arrays 21 hours. This file follows Week 2 of the study plan. Calendar dates are only in the 15-week plan . The gate is two parts: methods, text, and arrays questions at 70% (21 of the 30 questions in this file), and one hour with autocomplete off . Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Saturday and Sunday are 6 hours each. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 1 misses. The exam topic this week is text, String , StringBuilder , and arrays. Methods are the tool you use to write that code. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon Methods, return, overloading Chapter 1 through overloading, the Adder lab, questions Q1–Q4 Wed Varargs and pass-by-value The varargs overload, PassByValue , questions Q5–Q8 Fri String , StringBuilder , and a text block Chapter 2, TextCalls , the copy loop, questions Q1–Q8 Sat Arrays, sort, binarySearch Chapter 3, ArrayLab , questions Q1–Q8 Sun Autocomplete off for one hour, then the questions The hour, the mixed set, then score all 30 Same checklist as the study plan: Overload one method three ways, including varargs. Varargs is the last parameter, and there is only one. Prove pass-by-value: reassigning the parameter does not change the caller; mutating the object does. One call each for substring , indexOf , equals , strip , isBlank , indent , formatted , and a text block. The end index of substring is exclusive. Show, in a loop, why String concatenation copies and StringBuilder does not. One sentence in the notebook. Reverse an array in place, sort it, and binarySearch it. A negative result encodes the insertion point. Make one jagged two-dimensional array. Autocomplete off for one hour. Methods, text, and arrays questions at 70%. Classes, encapsulation, and constructors are Week 3. These listings use static methods so main can call them. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — Methods 必睇 參考書第 5 章 Methods：pp. 234–242（“Designing Methods”）、pp. 246–248（varargs）、pp. 266–279（“Passing Data among Methods”、“Overloading Methods”、Summary、Exam Essentials）。 唔使睇 “Applying Access Modifiers”（pp. 249–256）同 “Accessing Static Data”（pp. 257–265）留第 3 週。“Effectively Final Variables”（p. 244）留第 7 週。Review Questions（p. 280 起）唔使做。 Monday is return and overloading. Wednesday adds varargs and pass-by-value. The three overloads of add are one checklist item, finished on Wednesday. What you must know A method has a name, a parameter list, and a return type. The signature is the name plus the parameter types. Parameter names are not part of the signature. The return type is not part of the signature. static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } void returns nothing. A bare return; is legal in a void method. A method whose type is not void must return a value on every path that can finish. The compiler checks this. static int value(boolean flag) { if (flag) { return 1; } return 0; // without this line the method does not compile } Monday — overloading Two methods overload each other when they share a name and their parameter lists differ. A different return type alone does not overload. It does not compile. When more than one method could accept the call, Java picks in this order: Exact parameter types. Widen a primitive ( int to long ) . Box ( int to Integer ). After boxing, the value may widen to a supertype ( Integer to Object ). Varargs. Widening beats boxing. Boxing beats varargs. An int does not become a Long : that would widen and then box, and Java does not do that for a method call. int can box to Integer and then widen to Object . static void pick(int x) { System.out.println(\"int\"); } static void pick(long x) { System.out.println(\"long\"); } pick(1); // int, exact match If the int method is removed, pick(1) prints long . Widening is used before a method that takes Integer . Type the two-argument overloads on Monday. The varargs overload is Wednesday. public class Adder { static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } static double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(add(1, 2)); System.out.println(add(1.5, 2.5)); } } add(1, 2) prints 3 . add(1.5, 2.5) prints 4.0 . Monday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Wednesday — varargs A varargs parameter is zero or more arguments, and inside the method it is an array. It is the last parameter. A method has only one varargs parameter. static int add(int... nums) { int sum = 0; for (int n : nums) { sum += n; } return sum; } Add that method to Adder . Then call it. System.out.println(add(1, 2, 3)); // 6 System.out.println(add()); // 0, the array is empty, not null add(1, 2) still uses add(int, int) . The exact two-parameter method beats varargs. These do not compile: static void bad(int... nums, int extra) {} // varargs is not last static void same(int[] nums) {} static void same(int... nums) {} // int... and int[] are the same signature A call that matches two methods equally does not compile. show(1, 2) is ambiguous for show(int, long) and show(long, int) , because each needs one widening. pick(null) is ambiguous for pick(Integer) and pick(String) . Wednesday — pass-by-value Java copies the value of each argument into the parameter. For a primitive, the copy is the number. For an object, the copy is the reference. Reassigning the parameter changes the copy. Mutating the object changes the object the caller still points at. Type this and run it. public class PassByValue { static void reassign(String s) { s = \"changed\"; } static void mutate(StringBuilder sb) { sb.append(\"!\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { String name = \"Ada\"; reassign(name); System.out.println(name); StringBuilder word = new StringBuilder(\"cat\"); mutate(word); System.out.println(word); } } The run prints: Ada cat! name is still \"Ada\" because s = \"changed\" points the copy at a different object. word prints cat! because append changes the same StringBuilder the caller holds. Wednesday’s questions are Q5–Q8. Chapter 1 questions Q1–Q4 are Monday. Q5–Q8 are Wednesday. Q1. What is the result? public class Q1 { static int pick(int x) { return x; } static long pick(int x) { return x; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(pick(1)); } } A 1 B 1 printed as a long C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? public class Q2 { static void pick(int x) { System.out.print(\"int\"); } static void pick(long x) { System.out.print(\"long\"); } static void pick(Integer x) { System.out.print(\"Integer\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { pick(1); } } A int B long C Integer D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the output? public class Q3 { static void pick(long x) { System.out.print(\"long\"); } static void pick(Integer x) { System.out.print(\"Integer\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { pick(1); } } A long B Integer C longInteger D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the result of compiling this method? static int value(boolean flag) { if (flag) { return 1; } } A It compiles and returns 0 when flag is false B It compiles and returns 1 when flag is false C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown when flag is false 揭曉答案 Q5. Which method compiles? A static void m(int... a, int b) {} B static void m(int b, int... a) {} C Both D Neither 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the output? public class Q6 { static void m(int... a) { System.out.print(a.length); } public static void main(String[] args) { m(); m(1, 2); } } A 02 B 12 C null2 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the output? public class Q7 { static void reassign(int[] a) { a = new int[] {9}; } static void mutate(int[] a) { a[0] = 9; } public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = {1, 2}; reassign(nums); System.out.print(nums[0]); mutate(nums); System.out.print(nums[0]); } } A 11 B 19 C 99 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q8. What is the result? public class Q8 { static void pick(Integer a) { System.out.print(\"I\"); } static void pick(String a) { System.out.print(\"S\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { pick(null); } } A I B S C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Text 必睇 第 1 章 “Defining Text Blocks”（pp. 31–33，第 1 週跳過嘅）。第 4 章 pp. 166–188：“Creating and Manipulating Strings”、“Using the StringBuilder Class”、“Understanding Equality”。 唔使睇 “Working with Dates and Times”（p. 205 起）係第 5 週。Review Questions 唔使做。 Friday is this chapter, TextCalls , the concatenation loop, and questions Q1–Q8. What you must know A String cannot be changed. Every method that looks like it edits a string returns a new String . The variable still points at the old one until you assign the result. String s = \"animal\"; s.substring(0, 3); System.out.println(s); // animal s = s.substring(0, 3); System.out.println(s); // ani String literals with the same characters share one pooled object. new String(\"hi\") is a different object with the same characters. == compares references. equals compares characters. String a = \"hi\"; String b = \"hi\"; String c = new String(\"hi\"); System.out.println(a == b); // true System.out.println(a == c); // false System.out.println(a.equals(c)); // true The methods on the checklist Call Result to remember \"animal\".substring(1, 4) \"nim\" . The end index is exclusive. substring(3, 3) is \"\" . A begin past the end, or a begin after the end, throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException \"animal\".substring(3) \"mal\" . From that index to the end. substring(6) on a length-6 string is \"\" \"animals\".indexOf('a') 0 . The first match. A missing character or string returns -1 \"animals\".indexOf(\"al\") 4 \"hi\".equals(\"hi\") true . \"hi\".equals(null) is false . Calling equals on a null reference throws NullPointerException \" hi \".strip() \"hi\" . Also removes Unicode whitespace. trim only removes characters at or below U+0020 \" \".isBlank() true . Blank means empty or only whitespace \" \".isEmpty() false . Empty means length 0 \"a\".indent(1) \" a\\n\" . Each line is indented, line endings become \\n , and the result ends with \\n \"Hi %s\".formatted(\"Java\") \"Hi Java\" . Same idea as String.format charAt is the other index method. \"cat\".charAt(0) is 'c' . An index that does not exist throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException . length() is a method on a string. On an array, length is a field. The exam uses that difference. Friday lab — one call each Type this class. Predict each line, then run it. public class TextCalls { public static void main(String[] args) { String word = \"animal\"; System.out.println(word.substring(1, 4)); System.out.println(word.indexOf('a', 1)); System.out.println(word.equals(\"animal\")); System.out.println(\" hi \".strip()); System.out.println(\" \".isBlank()); System.out.println(\"a\".indent(1).equals(\" a\\n\")); System.out.println(\"Hi %s\".formatted(\"Java\")); String block = \"\"\" cat \"\"\"; System.out.println(block.equals(\"cat\\n\")); } } In that text block, cat and the closing \"\"\" start in the same column, so the spaces in the source are incidental and are removed. A text block is a String . The line breaks in the source become \\n , including the break before the closing quotes. \"cat\\n\" is the value. A text block cannot sit on one line. \"\"\"cat\"\"\" does not compile. The opening \"\"\" is the end of its line. The position of the closing \"\"\" sets how much shared indent is removed. Content that sticks out farther to the right keeps the extra spaces. StringBuilder StringBuilder is a changeable buffer. append , insert , delete , deleteCharAt , reverse , and replace change that buffer and return the same builder, so calls can chain. substring on a builder returns a String , so the chain cannot continue with append . StringBuilder does not override equals . sb.equals(\"hi\") is false even when the characters are hi . Compare sb.toString().equals(\"hi\") . var sb = new StringBuilder(\"animals\"); String start = sb.substring(0, 3); // \"ani\", and sb is still \"animals\" sb.append(\"!\"); // legal, sb is a builder // sb.substring(0, 3).append(\"x\"); // does not compile Friday — the copy loop Type this. Both variables end as \"012\" . Then write one sentence in the notebook: each += builds a new String and copies the characters so far; StringBuilder appends into one buffer. public class Copies { public static void main(String[] args) { String s = \"\"; for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { s += i; } StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(); for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { sb.append(i); } System.out.println(s.equals(sb.toString())); } } The print is true . The sentence is about the copies, not about the final characters. Chapter 2 questions Q1. What is the output? public class Q1 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(\"animal\".substring(1, 4)); } } A ani B nim C nima D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? public class Q2 { public static void main(String[] args) { String a = \"hi\"; String b = \"hi\"; String c = new String(\"hi\"); System.out.print(a == b); System.out.print(a == c); System.out.print(a.equals(c)); } } A truetruetrue B truefalsetrue C falsefalsetrue D truefalsefalse 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the output? public class Q3 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(\" \".isEmpty()); System.out.print(\" \".isBlank()); System.out.print(\"\".isBlank()); } } A falsetruetrue B falsefalsefalse C truetruetrue D falsetruefalse 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the output? public class Q4 { public static void main(String[] args) { var sb = new StringBuilder(\"hi\"); System.out.print(sb.equals(\"hi\")); System.out.print(sb.toString().equals(\"hi\")); } } A truetrue B falsetrue C falsefalse D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the result? public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { var sb = new StringBuilder(\"animals\"); sb.substring(0, 3).append(\"x\"); System.out.println(sb); } } A animals B anixals C xanimals D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the output? public class Q6 { public static void main(String[] args) { String s = \"animal\"; s.substring(0, 3); System.out.println(s); } } A ani B animal C mal D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the output? public class Q7 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(\"a\".indent(1).equals(\" a\\n\")); } } A true B false C a D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q8. What is the output? public class Q8 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(\"animals\".indexOf('z')); System.out.print(\" \"); System.out.print(\"animals\".indexOf(\"al\")); } } A -1 4 B 0 4 C -1 -1 D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Arrays 必睇 參考書第 4 章 “Understanding Arrays”，pp. 189–201。 唔使睇 Math 已在第 1 週。日期（p. 205 起）留第 5 週。Review Questions 唔使做。 Saturday is this chapter, ArrayLab , and questions Q1–Q8. What you must know An array is an object. The size is fixed when it is created. length is a field. int[] nums = new int[3]; // 0, 0, 0 String[] names = new String[2]; // null, null System.out.println(nums.length); nums.length() does not compile. A string uses length() with parentheses. An index from 0 through length - 1 is legal. Any other index throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at runtime. These create an array with values: int[] a = {1, 2, 3}; int[] b = new int[] {1, 2, 3}; The brace form without new is allowed only in the declaration. This does not compile: int[] c; c = {1, 2}; This does: c = new int[] {1, 2}; A field of array type defaults to null . Reading null.length throws NullPointerException . Sort, search, and a negative result Arrays.sort orders the array in place. Arrays.binarySearch is defined for a sorted array. On an unsorted array the result is undefined. The exam’s answer for that case is that the result is undefined, not a particular index. If the value is present, binarySearch returns its index. If it is absent, the result is negative: -(insertion point) - 1 . The insertion point is the index where the value would go to keep the array sorted. int[] sorted = {1, 3, 5, 7}; Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 5); // 2 Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 4); // -3, insertion point 2 Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 0); // -1, insertion point 0 For -3 , the insertion point is -(-3) - 1 , which is 2 . Arrays.equals compares elements one level deep. == compares the two array references. Arrays.mismatch returns the first index that differs, or -1 when the arrays match. Arrays.compare returns a negative number, zero, or a positive number, in the same style as compareTo . An array’s component type is checked at runtime. This compiles and throws ArrayStoreException : Object[] objects = new String[1]; objects[0] = 1; Saturday lab — reverse, sort, search, jagged Type this. Predict the prints, then run it. import java.util.Arrays; public class ArrayLab { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = {3, 1, 4, 2}; for (int i = 0; i < nums.length / 2; i++) { int tmp = nums[i]; nums[i] = nums[nums.length - 1 - i]; nums[nums.length - 1 - i] = tmp; } System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums)); Arrays.sort(nums); System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums)); System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(nums, 3)); System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(nums, 0)); int[][] jagged = {{1, 2}, {3}}; System.out.println(jagged.length); System.out.println(jagged[0].length); System.out.println(jagged[1].length); } } The run prints: [2, 4, 1, 3] [1, 2, 3, 4] 2 -1 2 2 1 The reverse swaps the ends and moves inward. After the sort, 3 is at index 2 . 0 would be inserted at index 0 , so the search returns -1 . jagged is an array of two arrays. The first inner array has length 2. The second has length 1. This also compiles, and the inner arrays are different lengths: int[][] rows = new int[2][]; rows[0] = new int[1]; rows[1] = new int[3]; rows[0] is null until you assign an array. rows[0][0] before that assignment throws NullPointerException . Chapter 3 questions Q1. What is the result? public class Q1 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = new int[3]; System.out.println(nums.length()); } } A 3 B 0 C The code does not compile D An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the result? public class Q2 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums; nums = {1, 2}; System.out.println(nums[0]); } } A 1 B 0 C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the output? import java.util.Arrays; public class Q3 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = {1, 3, 5, 7}; System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(nums, 4)); } } A 2 B -2 C -3 D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the result? import java.util.Arrays; public class Q4 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = {5, 1, 3}; System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(nums, 1)); } } A 1 B 0 C -1 D The result is undefined 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the result? public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = new int[2]; System.out.println(nums[0]); System.out.println(nums[2]); } } A 0 then 0 B 0 is printed, then an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the result? public class Q6 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[][] rows = new int[2][]; System.out.println(rows[0] == null); System.out.println(rows[1].length); } } A true then 0 B false then 0 C true is printed, then a NullPointerException is thrown D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the output? import java.util.Arrays; public class Q7 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] nums = {3, 1, 2}; Arrays.sort(nums); System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(nums, 2)); } } A 0 B 1 C 2 D -1 揭曉答案 Q8. What is the result? public class Q8 { public static void main(String[] args) { Object[] objects = new String[1]; objects[0] = 1; System.out.println(objects[0]); } } A 1 B null C The code does not compile D An ArrayStoreException is thrown 揭曉答案 Sunday — autocomplete off, then the questions 唔使睇 呢一小時關掉自動完成，然後做筆記嘅題。 Turn suggestions off for one hour: Settings, Editor, General, Code Completion, and clear “Show suggestions as you type”. Turn it back on when the hour ends. In that hour, write one program from a blank file. Use no checklist code copied from above. The program needs all three of these: an overloaded method, and one of the overloads is varargs a String or StringBuilder call that you predict before you run it a jagged int array, plus Arrays.sort and Arrays.binarySearch on a one-dimensional array About 40 lines is enough. If it compiles and the search result matches the insertion-point rule, the hour counts. Then do the mixed set. After that, score Chapter 1 (8), Chapter 2 (8), Chapter 3 (8), and the mixed set (6). 21 or more of the 30 questions is the gate. Every miss becomes one rule and a five-line program before Monday of Week 3. Mixed set M1. What is the output? public class M1 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.print(\"animal\".charAt(0)); System.out.print(\"animal\".substring(6)); } } A a B al C a followed by nothing else D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 M2. What is the output? public class M2 { static void grow(StringBuilder sb) { sb.append(\"s\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"cat\"); grow(sb); System.out.println(sb); } } A cat B cats C s D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M3. What is the output? import java.util.Arrays; public class M3 { public static void main(String[] args) { int[] a = {1, 2, 3}; int[] b = {1, 2, 4}; System.out.print(Arrays.mismatch(a, b)); System.out.print(Arrays.mismatch(a, a)); } } A 2-1 B 1-1 C 20 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M4. Which of these compile? String a = \"\"\"cat\"\"\"; String b = \"\"\" cat \"\"\"; A a only B b only C Both D Neither 揭曉答案 M5. What is the output? public class M5 { public static void main(String[] args) { var sb = new StringBuilder(\"abc\"); sb.append(\"de\").delete(1, 3).reverse(); System.out.println(sb); } } A abcde B eda C ade D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M6. What is the output? public class M6 { static void add(int a, int b) { System.out.print(\"two\"); } static void add(int... nums) { System.out.print(nums.length); } public static void main(String[] args) { add(1, 2); add(1, 2, 3); } } A two3 B 23 C two2 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card The signature is the name and the parameter types. A different return type does not overload. Call resolution: exact, then primitive widening, then boxing, then varargs. An int does not match a Long parameter. Varargs is the last parameter, and there is only one. Inside the method it is an array. A call with no extra arguments passes an empty array. Reassigning a parameter does not change the caller. Mutating the object the reference points at does. substring ’s end index is exclusive. indexOf returns -1 when there is no match. String methods do not change the original string. isBlank is true for empty or whitespace-only text. isEmpty is true only for length 0. StringBuilder.equals does not compare characters. substring on a builder returns a String . \"a\".indent(1) is \" a\\n\" . A text block’s closing \"\"\" decides the indent that is removed. Each += in a loop builds a new String . StringBuilder.append updates one buffer. length on an array is a field. binarySearch needs a sorted array. A missing value returns -(insertion point) - 1 ."}, {"href": "Week-02-glossary.html", "title": "Week 2 glossary — methods, text, and arrays", "hay": "Week 2 glossary — methods, text, and arrays Terms from Week-02-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — methods and overloading Term Meaning Signature The method name plus the parameter types. Parameter names are not part of it. The return type is not part of it. Overload Two methods with the same name and different parameter lists. A different return type alone is not an overload. It does not compile. Return path A non- void method must return a value on every path that can finish. A void method may use a bare return; . Overload resolution The order Java picks a method: exact match, then primitive widening, then boxing, then varargs. Widening beats boxing. Boxing beats varargs. Widening A primitive becomes a wider primitive, such as int to long . An int argument does not match a Long parameter, because that would widen and then box. Boxing in a call int can box to Integer , and that Integer can then widen to Object . Widening a primitive is tried before this boxing. Ambiguous call A call that matches two methods equally does not compile. show(1, 2) is ambiguous for show(int, long) and show(long, int) . pick(null) is ambiguous for pick(Integer) and pick(String) . Wednesday — varargs and pass-by-value Term Meaning Varargs A last parameter written int... nums . A method has only one. Inside the method it is an array. A call with no extra arguments passes an empty array, not null . int... and int[] The same signature. Declaring both does not compile. A parameter after the varargs does not compile. Exact match beats varargs add(int, int) is chosen for add(1, 2) when add(int...) also exists. add(1, 2, 3) can use only the varargs method. Pass-by-value Java copies each argument into the parameter. Reassigning the parameter changes the copy. Mutating the object the copy points at changes the object the caller still holds. Friday — String , StringBuilder , text blocks Term Meaning String immutability A String cannot be changed. Methods such as substring return a new String . The variable still points at the old one until you assign the result. String pool The same literal shares one object. new String(\"hi\") is a different object with the same characters. == and equals == compares references. equals compares characters. \"hi\".equals(null) is false . Calling equals on a null reference throws NullPointerException . substring The end index is exclusive. \"animal\".substring(1, 4) is nim . substring(3, 3) and substring(6) on a length-6 string are \"\" . A start past the length throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException . indexOf The index of the first match, or -1 when the character or string is missing. The search can start at a from-index. charAt The char at one index. A missing index throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException . length() A method on a String . An array uses the field length , with no parentheses. strip Removes leading and trailing whitespace, including Unicode whitespace. trim only removes characters at or below U+0020. isBlank True for empty text or text that is only whitespace. \" \".isBlank() is true. isEmpty True only when the length is 0. \" \".isEmpty() is false. The empty string is blank. indent Indents each line, turns line endings into \\n , and the result ends with \\n . \"a\".indent(1) equals \" a\\n\" . formatted Fills a format string. \"Hi %s\".formatted(\"Java\") is Hi Java . The same idea as String.format . Text block A String opened with \"\"\" at the end of a line. \"\"\"cat\"\"\" does not compile. The closing \"\"\" sets the indent that is removed. Content farther right keeps its extra spaces. A text block includes the newline before the closing quotes. StringBuilder A changeable buffer. append , insert , delete , deleteCharAt , reverse , and replace change that buffer and return it, so they can chain. delete The end index is exclusive. \"abcde\" after delete(1, 3) is ade . StringBuilder.substring Returns a String . The chain cannot continue with append . The builder itself is unchanged by that call. StringBuilder.equals Does not compare characters. It uses reference equality. Compare with toString().equals . += on a String Each += builds a new String and copies the characters so far. StringBuilder.append updates one buffer. Saturday — arrays Term Meaning Array An object with a fixed size. new int[3] holds 0, 0, 0 . new String[2] holds null, null . length A field. nums.length() does not compile. Legal indexes are 0 through length - 1 . Any other index throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException . Brace initializer {1, 2} without new is allowed only in the declaration. A later assignment needs new int[] {1, 2} . Array field Defaults to null . Reading null.length throws NullPointerException . Arrays.sort Sorts the array in place. Arrays.binarySearch Defined for a sorted array. A present value returns its index. A missing value returns -(insertion point) - 1 . On an unsorted array the result is undefined. Insertion point The index where a missing value would go to keep the array sorted. For a negative search result, the insertion point is -result - 1 . Arrays.equals Compares elements one level deep. == compares the two array references. Arrays.mismatch The first index that differs, or -1 when the arrays match. Arrays.compare A negative number, zero, or a positive number, in the same style as compareTo . ArrayStoreException Thrown when the variable type allows an assignment that the array’s runtime type rejects. Object[] objects = new String[1]; objects[0] = 1 throws it. Jagged array An array of arrays whose inner arrays may have different lengths. new int[2][] creates two slots and leaves them null . Using a null inner array throws NullPointerException ."}, {"href": "Week-03-notes.html", "title": "Week 3 notes — classes and memory", "hay": "Week 3 notes — classes and memory 21 hours. This file follows Week 3 of the study plan. Calendar dates are only in the 15-week plan . The gate is two parts: you can hand-trace superclass-then-subclass initializer order, and the class-design questions in this file at 70% (23 of the 32 questions). Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Saturday and Sunday are 6 hours each. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 2 misses. The exam topic this week is classes, encapsulation, and immutability, plus the garbage-collection diagrams on the checklist. Wednesday uses extends only so you can watch a superclass finish before the subclass. Overriding, interfaces, records, and sealed types are Week 4. Leave them until then. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon Classes, fields, constructors, this Chapter 1, Person , questions Q1–Q6 Wed Static members and initializer order Counter , Once , OrderLab , questions Q1–Q8 Fri Encapsulation OpenBox , BankAccount , questions Q1–Q6 Sat An immutable class and five garbage-collection diagrams Money , CopyLab , the five diagrams, questions Q1–Q6 Sun Chapter questions Hand-trace M1, the mixed set, then score all 32 Same checklist as the study plan: A class with fields, a constructor, and this . No public fields. Hand-write static-then-instance initializer order for a superclass and a subclass. Run code that prints the order and compare. An encapsulated BankAccount : private fields, constructor, methods. An immutable Money class: private final fields, no setters, class is final. Five memory diagrams. A box is an object, an arrow is a reference. Mark what is eligible for collection. Class-design questions at 70%, including one you can explain about garbage collection. Predict each listing before you run it. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — Classes, fields, constructors, this 必睇 參考書第 6 章 Class Design，pp. 297–311：“Creating Classes” 同 “Declaring Constructors”（ this 、 super() ）。 唔使睇 “Inheriting Members” 同 “Creating Abstract Classes” 留第 4 週。Review Questions 唔使做。 Monday is this chapter, the Person lab, and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know A class groups fields and methods. new Person(\"Ada\", 36) allocates an object and runs a constructor. The fields receive their defaults first: 0 for a number, false for a boolean, '\\u0000' for a char, and null for a reference. Those are the same defaults as Week 1. A local variable still has no default. A file has at most one public top-level class, and the file name matches that class. A top-level class is public or package-private (no modifier). private and protected are not modifiers for a top-level class. A constructor’s name is the class name, and it has no return type. public Person(String name) { this.name = name; } void Person(String name) is an ordinary method that happens to share the class name. new does not call it. If you write no constructor, the compiler adds a no-arg constructor with the same access as the class. The moment you write any constructor, that default constructor is gone. new Person() then fails unless one of your constructors takes no arguments. A parameter with the same name as a field hides the field. this.field is the field. n = n assigns the parameter to itself and leaves the field at its default. this(...) calls another constructor of the same class. It is the first statement of the constructor. The arguments cannot be instance fields or instance methods, because this object is not ready yet. A cycle, where two constructors call each other, does not compile. Instance initializers run in the constructor that actually builds the object. A constructor that starts with this(...) waits, and the body after this(...) runs when the other constructor returns. Type this. public class Chain { { System.out.print(\"I\"); } Chain() { this(1); System.out.print(\"A\"); } Chain(int n) { System.out.print(\"B\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { new Chain(); } } new Chain() prints IBA . The { } block and the Chain(int) body run inside the delegated constructor. A prints after that constructor returns. Monday lab — Person Type this. The fields are private. The constructor stores both parameters with this . public class Person { private String name; private int age; public Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; } public String describe() { return name + \" \" + age; } public static void main(String[] args) { Person p = new Person(\"Ada\", 36); System.out.println(p.describe()); } } The run prints: Ada 36 Monday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 1 questions Q1. What is the output? public class Q1 { boolean ok = true; void Q1() { ok = false; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new Q1().ok); } } A true B false C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? public class Q2 { int n = 5; void set(int n) { n = 10; } public static void main(String[] args) { Q2 q = new Q2(); q.set(10); System.out.println(q.n); } } A 5 B 10 C 0 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the result? public class Q3 { int n; Q3() { n = 1; this(2); } Q3(int n) { this.n = n; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new Q3().n); } } A 1 B 2 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the result? public class Q4 { Q4(int n) {} public static void main(String[] args) { new Q4(); } } A It runs and prints nothing B 0 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the result? public class Q5 { Q5() { this(1); } Q5(int n) { this(); } public static void main(String[] args) { new Q5(); } } A It runs and prints nothing B A StackOverflowError is thrown C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the result? public class Q6 { int n = 1; Q6() { this(n); } Q6(int n) {} public static void main(String[] args) { new Q6(); } } A It runs and prints nothing B 1 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Static members and initializer order 必睇 第 5 章 “Accessing Static Data”，pp. 257–265。第 6 章 “Initializing Objects”，pp. 312–319。 唔使睇 p. 320 起嘅 overriding 留第 4 週。 Wednesday is this chapter, three listings, and questions Q1–Q8. The hand-written order is a gate item. Write it on paper before you run OrderLab . What you must know A static member belongs to the class. There is one copy, shared by every object. An instance field belongs to one object. A static method can use static members. It cannot use this or an instance member, because it has no object. An instance method can use static members. A subclass extends one superclass. Constructors are not inherited. The child constructor calls a parent constructor first. If you write neither this(...) nor super(...) , the compiler inserts super() . That inserted call needs a no-arg constructor on the superclass. If the superclass only has Parent(int n) , the child must start with super(n) . Overriding is Week 4. Initialization of one class, the first time it is used: static fields and static blocks, top to bottom, once. For each new : instance fields and instance blocks, top to bottom, then the constructor body. A constructor that starts with this(...) does not repeat step 2. Step 2 runs in the constructor that calls the superclass. You already saw that as IBA in Chapter 1. The first time a subclass is used, the superclass goes first: Superclass static fields and blocks. Subclass static fields and blocks. Superclass instance fields and blocks. Superclass constructor body. Subclass instance fields and blocks. Subclass constructor body. The second new of that subclass skips steps 1 and 2. Both classes are already loaded. If main is inside the class you are loading, the static steps run before the first line of main . A blank final field is assigned exactly once on every constructor path. The assignment can be the declaration, an instance block, or the constructor body. Two assignments do not compile. Zero assignments do not compile. An initializer may call a method. It may not use a field that is declared later by its simple name. int a = b; // does not compile: illegal forward reference int b = 7; int a = read(); // compiles int b = 7; int read() { return b; } new of that second class prints 0 for a . The field b still holds its default when read() runs. b = 7 has not run yet. Counter Type this. Two objects share one static field. public class Counter { static int made; Counter() { made++; } public static void main(String[] args) { new Counter(); new Counter(); System.out.println(made); } } The run prints: 2 Once Predict the line, then type this. main is inside the class, so the static work runs before main prints the space. public class Once { static int s = mark(\"S1\"); static { mark(\"S2\"); } int i = mark(\"I1\"); { mark(\"I2\"); } Once() { mark(\"C\"); } static int mark(String label) { System.out.print(label); return 0; } public static void main(String[] args) { new Once(); System.out.print(\" \"); new Once(); System.out.println(); } } The run prints: S1S2I1I2C I1I2C S1S2 is stuck to the first object because those two marks happen before main starts. The second object prints I1I2C only. OrderLab On paper, list the six steps from the section above and write the output. Then type this and compare. The file name is OrderLab.java . class Parent { static { System.out.print(\"1\"); } int p = mark(\"3\"); { System.out.print(\"4\"); } Parent() { System.out.print(\"5\"); } static int mark(String s) { System.out.print(s); return 0; } } class Child extends Parent { static { System.out.print(\"2\"); } int c = mark(\"6\"); { System.out.print(\"7\"); } Child() { System.out.print(\"8\"); } } public class OrderLab { public static void main(String[] args) { new Child(); System.out.print(\" \"); new Child(); System.out.println(); } } The run prints: 12345678 345678 Digit What ran 1 Parent static block 2 Child static block 3 Parent instance field 4 Parent instance block 5 Parent constructor 6 Child instance field 7 Child instance block 8 Child constructor The second object starts at 3 . The static steps have already run. Wednesday’s questions are Q1–Q8. Chapter 2 questions Q1. What is the output? public class Q1 { static { System.out.print(\"S\"); } { System.out.print(\"I\"); } Q1() { System.out.print(\"C\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { new Q1(); } } A SIC B ISC C CIS D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? public class Q2 { static { System.out.print(\"S\"); } { System.out.print(\"I\"); } Q2() { System.out.print(\"C\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { new Q2(); new Q2(); } } A SICIC B SICSIC C ICIC D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the output? The file name is Q3.java . class Base { static { System.out.print(\"1\"); } { System.out.print(\"3\"); } Base() { System.out.print(\"4\"); } } public class Q3 extends Base { static { System.out.print(\"2\"); } { System.out.print(\"5\"); } Q3() { System.out.print(\"6\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { new Q3(); } } A 123456 B 123456123456 C 125634 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the result of compiling this class? public class Q4 { int n = 1; static void show() { System.out.println(n); } } A It compiles B It compiles, and show() prints 1 C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown when the class loads 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the output? public class Q5 { int a = read(); int b = 7; int read() { return b; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new Q5().a); } } A 0 B 7 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the result of compiling this class? public class Q6 { final int n; Q6() {} } A It compiles, and n is 0 B It compiles, and n is null C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown when new Q6() runs 揭曉答案 Q7. What is the result? The file name is Q7.java . class Parent { Parent(int n) {} } public class Q7 extends Parent { Q7() {} } A It compiles B It compiles, and new Q7() prints nothing C The code does not compile D A StackOverflowError is thrown 揭曉答案 Q8. What is the output? public class Q8 { { System.out.print(\"X\"); } Q8() { this(1); System.out.print(\"Y\"); } Q8(int n) { System.out.print(\"Z\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { new Q8(); } } A XYZ B XZY C ZXY D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Encapsulation 必睇 第 5 章 “Applying Access Modifiers”，pp. 249–256。 唔使睇 第 7 章 “Understanding Encapsulation”（p. 387）係 record，留第 4 週。 Friday is this chapter, OpenBox , BankAccount , and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know Encapsulation means the data is private and every change goes through a method. Another class cannot assign a bad value to the field, because it cannot name the field. Modifier Who can use the member private The declaring class only no modifier Classes in the same package protected The same package, and subclasses public Every class Week 4 practices a subclass that lives in another package. This week, fields of a bank account are private . private still allows the class’s own main to read the field. The ban applies to other classes. A package-private member, the one with no modifier, is visible to another class in the same file, because that file is the same package. A getter that returns a String is safe. A String cannot be changed. A getter that returns a StringBuilder , an array, or a list hands the caller the object you stored. The caller can append or replace elements, and your field sees the change. Return a copy when the stored object is mutable. OpenBox Type this. The file name is OpenBox.java . class Box { int size = 2; private int secret = 9; } public class OpenBox { public static void main(String[] args) { Box box = new Box(); System.out.println(box.size); // box.secret = 1; } } The run prints: 2 size has no modifier, and OpenBox is in the same package, so the read is legal. Remove the comment from box.secret and the file does not compile. secret is private to Box . BankAccount Type this. Both fields are private. deposit and withdraw change cents only when the amount is one the account can accept. A non-positive deposit is ignored. A withdrawal larger than the balance is ignored. Week 5 replaces that quiet ignore with an exception. public class BankAccount { private String owner; private int cents; public BankAccount(String owner, int cents) { this.owner = owner; this.cents = cents; } public String owner() { return owner; } public int cents() { return cents; } public void deposit(int amount) { if (amount > 0) { cents += amount; } } public void withdraw(int amount) { if (amount > 0 && amount <= cents) { cents -= amount; } } public static void main(String[] args) { BankAccount account = new BankAccount(\"Ada\", 100); account.deposit(40); account.withdraw(25); account.withdraw(1000); System.out.println(account.cents()); } } Predict the balance, then run. The print is: 115 100 + 40 is 140 , then 25 leaves 115 . The withdrawal of 1000 does not run, because 1000 is greater than 115 . Friday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 3 questions Q1. Which class is encapsulated? class A { public int cents; } class B { private int cents; public int cents() { return cents; } } A A only B B only C Both D Neither 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? public class Q2 { private int cents; public void setCents(int cents) { cents = cents; } public int cents() { return cents; } public static void main(String[] args) { Q2 q = new Q2(); q.setCents(50); System.out.println(q.cents()); } } A 50 B 0 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the result? The file name is Q3.java . class Secret { private int n = 1; int size = 2; } public class Q3 { public static void main(String[] args) { Secret s = new Secret(); System.out.println(s.n); } } A 1 B 2 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the output? public class Q4 { private final StringBuilder name = new StringBuilder(\"a\"); public StringBuilder name() { return name; } public static void main(String[] args) { Q4 q = new Q4(); q.name().append(\"b\"); System.out.println(q.name()); } } A a B ab C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the output? public class Q5 { private int cents = 100; public void deposit(int amount) { if (amount > 0) { cents += amount; } } public static void main(String[] args) { Q5 q = new Q5(); q.deposit(-40); System.out.println(q.cents); } } A 60 B 100 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q6. Which assignment compiles, when the assignment is written in a third class? class Account { public int cents; } class Safe { private int cents; } A new Account().cents = -1 only B new Safe().cents = -1 only C Both D Neither 揭曉答案 Chapter 4 — Immutability and garbage collection 必睇 第 6 章 “Creating Immutable Objects”（在 “Creating Abstract Classes” 之後、Summary 之前，包括 defensive copy）。第 1 章 “Destroying Objects”（在該章 Summary 之前）。 唔使睇 “Creating Abstract Classes” 本身留第 4 週，呢個星期跳過。 Saturday is this chapter, Money , CopyLab , five diagrams, and questions Q1–Q6. Drawing the diagrams is a checklist item. Draw each one before you read its mark. What you must know An immutable object does not change after the constructor finishes. The usual shape is: the class is final , so a subclass cannot add mutable behavior every field is private and final there is no setter a method that looks like a change returns a new object a field that points at a mutable object is a copy, both in the constructor and in the getter final fixes the reference stored in the field. It does not freeze the StringBuilder , array, or list that the reference points at. A String or an int has nothing further to change, so a final field of that kind is safe to share. An object is eligible for collection when no live reference can reach it. Eligible means the collector is allowed to reclaim it. The exam asks about eligibility. It does not ask you to name the moment the collector actually runs. These references keep an object alive: a local variable that still holds it a field of a reachable object a static field Setting that variable to null , pointing it at a different object, or leaving the block where it was declared drops that one reference. The object becomes eligible only when the last reference is gone. Two objects that point at each other, with no reference from outside, are both eligible. That is an island. System.gc() asks for a collection. A reachable object stays. The call returns without a promise that anything was collected. Money Type this. The class is final . Both fields are private final . plus returns a new Money and leaves price alone. This lab uses the currency of the object you called plus on. public final class Money { private final int cents; private final String currency; public Money(int cents, String currency) { this.cents = cents; this.currency = currency; } public int cents() { return cents; } public String currency() { return currency; } public Money plus(Money other) { return new Money(this.cents + other.cents, this.currency); } public static void main(String[] args) { Money price = new Money(100, \"USD\"); Money total = price.plus(new Money(50, \"USD\")); System.out.println(price.cents()); System.out.println(total.cents()); } } The run prints: 100 150 CopyLab Predict the line, then type this. The file name is CopyLab.java . Shared stores the caller’s builder. Copied stores a new builder with the same characters. final class Shared { private final StringBuilder name; Shared(StringBuilder name) { this.name = name; } public String toString() { return name.toString(); } } final class Copied { private final StringBuilder name; Copied(StringBuilder name) { this.name = new StringBuilder(name); } public String toString() { return name.toString(); } } public class CopyLab { public static void main(String[] args) { StringBuilder raw = new StringBuilder(\"a\"); Shared shared = new Shared(raw); raw.append(\"b\"); System.out.print(shared); System.out.print(\" \"); StringBuilder raw2 = new StringBuilder(\"a\"); Copied copied = new Copied(raw2); raw2.append(\"b\"); System.out.println(copied); } } The run prints: ab a Shared changed because raw and the field are the same object. Copied still prints a . Five diagrams A box is an object. An arrow is a reference. Draw all five on paper and mark which boxes are eligible. Then read the marks. Diagram 1. A local variable. static void run() { Object obj = new Object(); } Diagram 2. Two names, then one is cleared. Object a = new Object(); Object b = a; a = null; Diagram 3. One name is pointed at a new object. Object a = new Object(); a = new Object(); Diagram 4. An island. class Node { Node next; } Node a = new Node(); Node b = new Node(); a.next = b; b.next = a; a = null; b = null; Diagram 5. A static field. class Hold { static Object kept; static void run() { kept = new Object(); Object local = kept; local = null; } } Draw before the marks below. Diagram 1 mark. Inside run , obj points at the box, so it is not eligible. After run returns, that local variable is gone. Nothing else was stored. The box is eligible. Diagram 2 mark. After a = null , b still points at the box. The box is not eligible. Diagram 3 mark. The first box has no arrow left. It is eligible. a points at the second box, so the second box is not eligible. Diagram 4 mark. After a = null and b = null , the two boxes point at each other and nothing outside points in. Both are eligible. Diagram 5 mark. After run returns, local is gone and kept still points at the box. The box is not eligible. It stays reachable until kept is changed. Saturday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 4 questions Q1. What is the output? The file name is Q1.java . final class Bag { private final StringBuilder items; Bag(StringBuilder items) { this.items = items; } public String toString() { return items.toString(); } } public class Q1 { public static void main(String[] args) { StringBuilder items = new StringBuilder(\"a\"); Bag bag = new Bag(items); items.append(\"b\"); System.out.println(bag); } } A a B ab C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? The file name is Q2.java . final class Coin { private final int cents; Coin(int cents) { this.cents = cents; } int cents() { return cents; } Coin plus(Coin other) { return new Coin(cents + other.cents); } } public class Q2 { public static void main(String[] args) { Coin a = new Coin(100); Coin b = a.plus(new Coin(50)); System.out.println(a.cents()); System.out.println(b.cents()); } } A 100 then 150 B 150 then 150 C 100 then 100 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. Which class is immutable? final class A { private final int cents; A(int cents) { this.cents = cents; } A plus(int more) { return new A(cents + more); } } final class B { private final StringBuilder name; B(StringBuilder name) { this.name = name; } StringBuilder name() { return name; } } A A only B B only C Both D Neither 揭曉答案 Q4. After a = null , and before main returns, is the object eligible? public class Q4 { public static void main(String[] args) { Object a = new Object(); Object b = a; a = null; } } A Yes, because a was set to null B No, because b still points at it C Yes, but only if the method calls System.gc() D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q5. After both assignments to null , which objects are eligible? The file name is Q5.java . class Node { Node next; } public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { Node a = new Node(); Node b = new Node(); a.next = b; b.next = a; a = null; b = null; } } A Neither, because each next still points at a node B Both C Only the node that a used to reference D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q6. What is true after System.gc() returns? public class Q6 { static Object kept; public static void main(String[] args) { kept = new Object(); System.gc(); } } A The object has been collected B The object is still reachable through kept C System.gc() does not compile D The call makes the object eligible 揭曉答案 Sunday — chapter questions 唔使睇 用手追溯 initializer 順序，然後用筆記計分。 The first 20 minutes are Saturday’s misses. Then write the output of M1 on paper before you read the mixed answers. That tracing is the other half of the gate. Use the six steps from Chapter 2. The second new skips the static steps. Then do M2–M6. After that, score Chapter 1 (6), Chapter 2 (8), Chapter 3 (6), Chapter 4 (6), and the mixed set (6). 23 or more of the 32 questions is the score half of the gate. The trace half is a correct M1, written before you check it. Every miss becomes one rule and a five-line program before Monday of Week 4. Say M4 out loud before you check it. Name the two objects, say which arrows remain after first = second , and say which object is eligible. A correct letter with no explanation does not finish the checklist item. Mixed set M1. What is the output? Write it before you look at the answer. The file name is M1.java . class Base { static { System.out.print(\"A\"); } { System.out.print(\"C\"); } Base() { System.out.print(\"D\"); } } public class M1 extends Base { static { System.out.print(\"B\"); } { System.out.print(\"E\"); } M1() { System.out.print(\"F\"); } public static void main(String[] args) { new M1(); new M1(); } } A ABCDEFCDEF B ABCDEFABCDEF C CDEFCDEF D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M2. What is the output? public class M2 { static int made; int n; M2(int n) { this.n = n; made++; } public static void main(String[] args) { M2 a = new M2(1); M2 b = new M2(2); System.out.print(a.n); System.out.print(made); } } A 12 B 22 C 11 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M3. What is the output? The file name is M3.java . final class Tag { private final StringBuilder text; Tag(StringBuilder text) { this.text = new StringBuilder(text); } public String toString() { return text.toString(); } } public class M3 { public static void main(String[] args) { StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder(\"a\"); Tag tag = new Tag(text); text.append(\"b\"); System.out.println(tag); } } A a B ab C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 M4. What is true after first = second ? Explain this one out loud. public class M4 { public static void main(String[] args) { Object first = new Object(); Object second = new Object(); first = second; } } A Both objects are eligible B The first object is eligible, and the second is not C The second object is eligible, and the first is not D Neither object is eligible 揭曉答案 M5. What is the output? public class M5 { private int cents = 80; public void withdraw(int amount) { if (amount > 0 && amount <= cents) { cents -= amount; } } public int cents() { return cents; } public static void main(String[] args) { M5 m = new M5(); m.withdraw(80); m.withdraw(1); System.out.println(m.cents()); } } A 80 B 0 C -1 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 M6. What is the output? public class M6 { final int n; { n = 3; } M6() {} public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new M6().n); } } A 0 B 3 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card A constructor has the class name and no return type. A return type makes it a method. Any constructor you write removes the default no-arg constructor. this.field is the field. A parameter of the same name hides the field. this(...) is the first statement. Its arguments cannot be instance members. A cycle of constructors does not compile. static is one per class and runs once, when the class is first used. A static method cannot use instance members. For one class: static fields and blocks in source order, then instance fields and blocks in source order, then the constructor body. Superclass, then subclass: parent static, child static, parent instance, parent constructor, child instance, child constructor. The second new skips the static steps. int a = read() can see the default 0 when b ’s initializer has not run. int a = b before b is declared does not compile. A blank final is assigned once on every constructor path. If the superclass has no no-arg constructor, the subclass starts with super(...) . private is the class. No modifier is the package. protected is the package and subclasses. public is everywhere. Encapsulated fields are private. A getter that returns a mutable object lets the caller change it. An immutable class is final , with private final fields, no setters, and no shared mutable object. A change returns a new object. Eligible means unreachable. One live reference keeps the object, including a static field. An island with no outside reference is eligible. System.gc() is a request."}, {"href": "Week-03-glossary.html", "title": "Week 3 glossary — classes and memory", "hay": "Week 3 glossary — classes and memory Terms from Week-03-notes.md . The exam words are English. Overriding, interfaces, records, and sealed types are Week 4. Monday — constructors and this Term Meaning Constructor A block whose name is the class name and which has no return type. new allocates the object and runs a constructor. void Person() is a method. new does not call it. Default constructor The no-arg constructor the compiler adds only when you write none. It has the same access as the class. The first constructor you write removes it. Top-level class public or package-private. A file has at most one public top-level class, and the file name matches it. private and protected are not allowed on a top-level class. this.field The field. A parameter of the same name hides the field. n = n assigns the parameter to itself and leaves the field at its default. this(...) A call to another constructor of the same class. It is the first statement. Its arguments cannot be instance fields or instance methods. A cycle of constructors does not compile. Instance initializer A { } block in the class body. It runs in the constructor that actually builds the object, not in a constructor that starts with this(...) . Wednesday — static and initialization order Term Meaning static member One copy for the class, shared by every object. A static method cannot use this or an instance member. An instance method can use a static member. static initializer A static { } block. It runs once, when the class is first used. If main is inside that class, it runs before the first line of main . Initialization order For one class: static fields and blocks in source order, once. Then each new runs instance fields and blocks in source order, then the constructor body. Superclass, then subclass Parent static, child static, parent instance, parent constructor, child instance, child constructor. The second new skips the static steps. super() The parent constructor call. If you write neither this(...) nor super(...) , the compiler inserts super() . That call needs a no-arg constructor on the parent. Constructors are not inherited. Blank final A final instance field with no initializer. It is assigned once on every constructor path: in the declaration, an instance block, or the constructor. A second assignment does not compile. Illegal forward reference int a = b does not compile when b is declared later. int a = read() can compile and see b ’s default 0 , because b ’s initializer has not run yet. Friday — encapsulation Term Meaning Encapsulation Fields are private, and every change goes through a method. Another class cannot assign the field. private Visible in the declaring class only, including that class’s own main . Package-private No modifier. Visible to classes in the same package. Classes in the same file share a package. protected Visible in the same package, and in subclasses. A subclass in another package is practiced in Week 4. public Visible to every class. Mutable escape A getter that returns a StringBuilder , an array, or a list hands out the object you stored. The caller can change it. Return a copy. A returned String is safe, because a String cannot be changed. Saturday — immutability and garbage collection Term Meaning Immutable class final , with private final fields, no setters, and no shared mutable object. A method that looks like a change returns a new object. final reference The field cannot be pointed at a different object. The StringBuilder or list it already points at can still change. Copy on the way in and on the way out. Eligible No live reference can reach the object, so the collector is allowed to reclaim it. Eligible is not the same as already collected. Live reference A local variable that still holds the object, a field of a reachable object, or a static field. One remaining reference keeps the object alive. Island Two or more objects that point only at each other. With no reference from outside, all of them are eligible. System.gc() A request for a collection. It does not make a reachable object eligible, and it does not promise that anything was collected."}, {"href": "Week-04-notes.html", "title": "Week 4 notes — inheritance through pattern matching", "hay": "Week 4 notes — inheritance through pattern matching 25 hours. Weekend days are 8 hours. This file follows Week 4 of the study plan. Calendar dates are only in the 15-week plan . The gate is the mixed OOP questions at 70% (21 of the 30 questions in this file). Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Saturday and Sunday are 8 hours each. The extra two hours are questions. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 3 misses. This is the heavy week before the holidays. The exam topic is inheritance, interfaces, records, sealed types, and patterns. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon extends , super , overriding rules on one card Chapter 1, Zoo , the overriding card, questions Q1–Q6 Wed Reference type versus object type, and field hiding Chapter 2, questions Q1–Q6 Fri equals , hashCode , interfaces, and enums Chapter 3, Coins , Pets , Season , questions Q1–Q6 Sat (8 h) Records, sealed types, switch patterns, record patterns Chapter 4, Coin , Shapes , questions Q1–Q6 Sun (8 h) Nested-class rules in the notebook, then a mixed OOP set The notebook page, the mixed set, then score all 30 Same checklist as the study plan: Animal , Dog , and Cat . Overriding rules on one card: signature, covariant return, access, checked exceptions. One example where a field is hidden, not overridden. The reference type decides what compiles; the object type decides which override runs. A correct equals and hashCode , and one broken equals . If equals is true, the hash codes match. An interface with a default method and a static method, two implementers, and an enum with a field and a method. A sealed interface with an exhaustive switch, a record with a compact constructor, a record pattern, and a when guard. A general pattern above a specific one does not compile. Mixed questions on inheritance, interfaces, records, and patterns at 70%. Nested classes: one notebook page on what each kind can see. Predict each listing before you run it. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — extends , super , and overriding 必睇 第 6 章 pp. 292–296（“Understanding Inheritance”）、pp. 320–329（“Inheriting Members”），同 “Creating Abstract Classes”（緊接其後，到 “Creating Immutable Objects” 之前）。 唔使睇 第 7 章留到本章之後幾日。Review Questions 唔使做。 Monday is this chapter, Zoo , the overriding card, and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know A class extends one superclass. Constructors are not inherited. The first line of a child constructor is super(...) or this(...) . If you write neither, the compiler inserts super() . An override replaces an instance method for objects of the subclass. Copy this card into the notebook: The signature matches: the same name and the same parameter types. A different parameter list is an overload, not an override. The return type is the same, or a subtype of the parent’s return type. That is a covariant return. The access is the same or wider. public cannot become package-private. A checked exception is the same, a subtype, or absent. The child cannot throw a broader checked exception, and it cannot add a new one. private , static , and final methods are not overridden. A static method with the same signature is hidden. A final method cannot be redeclared. A private method is not visible, so a child method of the same name is a new method. @Override asks the compiler to check that a real override exists. Put it on every override you type this week. super.speak() calls the parent version from inside the child. An abstract class can be extended and cannot be constructed with new . A method marked abstract has no body. The class that declares it must itself be abstract . A concrete subclass implements every abstract method, or that subclass is abstract too. An abstract class may still have fields, constructors, and ordinary methods. The child constructor still starts with super(...) . abstract cannot be combined with final , and an abstract method cannot be private or static . Monday lab — Zoo Type this. The file name is Zoo.java . class Animal { String kind = \"animal\"; String speak() { return \"generic\"; } } class Dog extends Animal { String kind = \"dog\"; @Override String speak() { return \"woof\"; } } class Cat extends Animal { String kind = \"cat\"; @Override String speak() { return \"meow\"; } } public class Zoo { public static void main(String[] args) { Animal dog = new Dog(); Animal cat = new Cat(); System.out.println(dog.kind + \" \" + dog.speak()); System.out.println(cat.kind + \" \" + cat.speak()); } } The run prints: animal woof animal meow kind is a field, so it is hidden. The reference type Animal selects animal . speak is overridden. The object type selects woof or meow . Wednesday uses this listing again. Monday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 1 questions Q1. Does this compile? class Animal { Animal create() { return new Animal(); } } class Dog extends Animal { @Override Dog create() { return new Dog(); } } A Yes. The return type is covariant B Yes, but only if create is static C No. The return type must be identical D No. Dog cannot extend Animal 揭曉答案 Q2. Does this compile? class Animal { public void speak() {} } class Dog extends Animal { void speak() {} } A Yes B Yes, because package access is the default C No. The override is less accessible D No. void methods cannot be overridden 揭曉答案 Q3. Does this compile? import java.io.*; class Animal { void speak() throws IOException {} } class Dog extends Animal { void speak() throws Exception {} } A Yes. Exception includes IOException B Yes, because the method is an override C No. The checked exception is broader D No. speak must be public 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the output? class Animal { static String speak() { return \"animal\"; } } class Dog extends Animal { static String speak() { return \"dog\"; } } public class Q4 { public static void main(String[] args) { Animal a = new Dog(); System.out.println(a.speak()); } } A dog B animal C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the output? The file name is Q5.java . class Animal { private String speak() { return \"animal\"; } String call() { return speak(); } } class Dog extends Animal { public String speak() { return \"dog\"; } } public class Q5 { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new Dog().call()); } } A dog B animal C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q6. Does this compile? class Animal { void speak() {} } class Dog extends Animal { @Override void speak(int n) {} } A Yes. speak(int) overrides speak() B Yes. @Override allows a new parameter C No. The signature does not match, so this is not an override D No. A subclass cannot declare speak 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Reference type and object type 必睇 第 7 章 “Understanding Polymorphism”，pp. 410–418。如果星期一未讀 “Hiding Variables”（p. 328），補嗰一頁。 唔使睇 唔好由第 7 章開頭順住讀。interfaces、enums、sealed、records、nested classes 係後面幾日。 Wednesday is this chapter and questions Q1–Q6. Use Zoo from Monday as the hiding example on the checklist. What you must know The reference type is the type on the left of the variable. The object type is the class after new . The reference type decides which names compile. If fetch is declared only on Dog , Animal a = new Dog(); a.fetch() does not compile. A cast compiles when the type is in the hierarchy. A cast to the wrong object throws ClassCastException at runtime. The object type decides which override runs. Fields do not work that way. A field in the subclass hides the parent field. The reference type selects the field, including through a cast: ((Dog) a).kind reads Dog ’s field. instanceof tests the object type. null instanceof Dog is false . It does not throw. A pattern both tests and assigns: if (a instanceof Dog d) { System.out.println(d.kind); } Inside that block, d has type Dog . Chapter 2 questions Q1. Using Animal , Dog , and Cat from Zoo , what is the output? Animal dog = new Dog(); System.out.println(dog.kind + \" \" + dog.speak()); A dog woof B animal woof C animal generic D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. What is the output? Animal dog = new Dog(); System.out.println(((Dog) dog).kind); A animal B dog C The code does not compile D A ClassCastException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q3. Dog declares void fetch() . Animal does not. What is the result? Animal a = new Dog(); a.fetch(); A It runs Dog.fetch B The code does not compile C A ClassCastException is thrown D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q4. What is the output? System.out.println(null instanceof Dog); A true B false C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q5. What is the output? Dog.kind is \"dog\" . Animal a = new Dog(); if (a instanceof Dog d) { System.out.println(d.kind); } A animal B dog C The code does not compile D Nothing is printed 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the result? Animal a = new Animal(); Dog d = (Dog) a; A d refers to an Animal B The code does not compile C A ClassCastException is thrown D d is null 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — equals , hashCode , interfaces, and enums 必睇 第 4 章 “Understanding Equality”，pp. 186–188（第 2 週讀過 String ，今日重讀 equals / hashCode 契約）。第 7 章 pp. 360–379：“Implementing Interfaces” 同 “Working with Enums”。private interface method 在 “Declaring Concrete Interface Methods”（p. 367）。 唔使睇 sealed 同 record 係星期六。 Friday is this chapter, three listings, and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know equals is reflexive, symmetric, transitive, and consistent. x.equals(null) is false . If a.equals(b) is true , then a.hashCode() and b.hashCode() return the same int . The reverse is not required: equal hash codes do not mean equals is true. A broken equals overrides equals and leaves hashCode as the identity hash from Object . A HashSet may then hold two objects that say they are equal. This run’s broken set has size 2. The fixed pair has size 1. Coins Type this. The file name is Coins.java . import java.util.*; class Bad { int n; Bad(int n) { this.n = n; } public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Bad b && b.n == n; } } class Good { int n; Good(int n) { this.n = n; } public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Good g && g.n == n; } public int hashCode() { return n; } } public class Coins { public static void main(String[] args) { Set<Bad> bad = new HashSet<>(); bad.add(new Bad(1)); bad.add(new Bad(1)); Set<Good> good = new HashSet<>(); good.add(new Good(1)); good.add(new Good(1)); System.out.println(bad.size() + \" \" + good.size()); } } The run prints: 2 1 Pets An interface can have a default instance method and a static method. The static method is called on the interface name. It is not inherited. interface Pet { String name(); default String speak() { return \"pet\"; } static String home() { return \"home\"; } } class DogPet implements Pet { public String name() { return \"Ada\"; } } class CatPet implements Pet { public String name() { return \"Bea\"; } @Override public String speak() { return \"meow\"; } } public class Pets { public static void main(String[] args) { Pet dog = new DogPet(); Pet cat = new CatPet(); System.out.println(dog.speak() + \" \" + cat.speak() + \" \" + Pet.home()); } } The run prints: pet meow home DogPet.home() does not compile. Two interfaces with the same default method force the class to override that method. A private method in an interface has a body and is visible only inside that interface. Implementing classes do not inherit it and cannot call it. A private instance method can be called from a default method. A private static method can be called from a static method or a default method. A static method cannot call a private instance method, because a static method has no instance. interface Steps { private static String tag(String s) { return \"[\" + s + \"]\"; } default String go() { return tag(\"go\"); } } class Walk implements Steps {} new Walk().go() returns [go] . Steps.tag(\"go\") and Walk.tag(\"go\") do not compile. The private method never leaves the interface. Season An enum constant list ends before the other members. The constructor runs once for each constant and is private. ordinal starts at 0. new Season() does not compile. enum Season { WINTER(\"cold\"), SUMMER(\"hot\"); private final String feel; Season(String feel) { this.feel = feel; } String feel() { return feel; } } public class Seasons { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(Season.WINTER.feel()); System.out.println(Season.SUMMER.ordinal()); } } The run prints: cold 1 Friday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 3 questions Q1. What is new Good(1).equals(null) using Good from Coins ? A true B false C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q2. Which statement matches the contract? A Equal hash codes mean equals returns true B If equals returns true , the hash codes match C hashCode must return the field n D equals may return true for null 揭曉答案 Q3. What does Coins print? A 1 1 B 2 2 C 2 1 D 1 2 揭曉答案 Q4. What does Pets print? A pet pet home B pet meow home C meow meow home D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q5. Does DogPet.home() compile? A Yes, and it returns home B Yes, and it returns pet C No. An interface static method is called on the interface D No. Pet has no static method 揭曉答案 Q6. What is the result of new Season(\"warm\") ? A A new constant named warm B The code does not compile C WINTER D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Chapter 4 — Records, sealed types, and patterns 必睇 第 7 章 pp. 380–399：“Sealing Classes” 同 “Encapsulating Data with Records”。第 3 章第 1 週跳過嘅兩節：pp. 106–110（ instanceof pattern）同 pp. 124–129（ switch pattern）。 唔使睇 nested classes 係星期日。 Saturday is 8 hours: this chapter, three listings, and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know A record is a final class with one field per component, a canonical constructor, accessors named after the components, and generated equals , hashCode , and toString . The accessor is cents() , not getCents() . A compact constructor has the record’s name and no parameter list. You assign the component parameters. The canonical constructor then assigns the fields. Use it to check or fix the values. public Coin { if (cents < 0) cents = 0; } A sealed type lists its permitted subtypes with permits . Each direct subtype is final , sealed , or non-sealed . A record that implements a sealed interface is implicitly final . A switch on a sealed type is exhaustive when every permitted type has a case. A default is then optional. A type pattern is case String s . A record pattern takes the components: case Point(int x, int y) . A when guard adds a boolean condition. Patterns are tested from top to bottom. A case that can never run, because an earlier case already covers it, does not compile. That is a dominated pattern. Put the specific pattern first. case null handles a null selector. Without case null , a null selector throws NullPointerException . Coin public record Coin(int cents) { public Coin { if (cents < 0) cents = 0; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new Coin(-5).cents()); } } The run prints: 0 Shapes The file name is Shapes.java . sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rect {} record Circle(int r) implements Shape {} record Rect(int w, int h) implements Shape {} public class Shapes { static String describe(Shape s) { return switch (s) { case Circle c -> \"circle\" + c.r(); case Rect r -> \"rect\"; }; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(describe(new Circle(3))); } } The run prints: circle3 There is no default . The sealed permits list makes the switch exhaustive. Record pattern and when record Point(int x, int y) {} public class Points { static String where(Object o) { return switch (o) { case Point(int x, int y) when x > 0 -> \"right\"; case Point(int x, int y) -> \"other\"; case null -> \"null\"; default -> \"no\"; }; } public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(where(new Point(2, 1))); System.out.println(where(new Point(-1, 4))); System.out.println(where(\"no\")); } } The run prints: right other no This dominated switch does not compile. The String case can never run: return switch (o) { case CharSequence c -> 1; case String s -> 2; default -> 3; }; Swap the two patterns and it compiles. String is more specific than CharSequence . Saturday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 4 questions Q1. What is new Coin(-5).cents() ? A -5 B 0 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q2. record Coin(int cents) {} . Does new Coin(1).getCents() compile? A Yes, and it returns 1 B Yes, and it returns 0 C No. The accessor is cents() D No. A record cannot have an int component 揭曉答案 Q3. What does Shapes print? A circle3 B rect C The code does not compile, because the switch has no default D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q4. Does the dominated switch in this chapter compile? A Yes, and a String selects case 2 B Yes, and a String selects case 1 C No. case String is dominated by case CharSequence D No. switch cannot use CharSequence 揭曉答案 Q5. What does where(new Point(2, 1)) print? A right B other C no D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q6. What does where(null) print in Points ? A null B no C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Sunday — nested classes, then the questions 必睇 第 7 章 “Creating Nested Classes”，pp. 400–409。 唔使睇 第 7 章 Review Questions。混合題用筆記計分。 The first 20 minutes are Saturday’s misses. Then copy this page into the notebook. Then do the mixed set and score all 30. 21 or more is the gate. Notebook page — what each nested class can see Static nested class. Declared with static inside another class. It has no hidden outer instance. It can use the outer class’s private static members. It can use an instance member only through an outer object you pass in. Create it with new Outer.Nested() . Inner class. A member class without static . Each inner object has a hidden reference to an outer object. Create it with outer.new Inner() , or with new Inner() inside an instance method of the outer class. It can read the outer class’s private instance members. Outer.this is the outer object. On Java 21 an inner class may declare static members. Local class. Declared inside a method. It has no access modifier. It can use the enclosing class’s members, and it can use local variables and parameters that are final or effectively final. Anonymous class. An expression with no name. It extends one class or implements one interface. It has no constructor of its own. An instance initializer is allowed. It can use effectively final locals, the same way a local class can. Mixed set M1. An outer class has private int n = 4 , and its inner class method returns n . Does the inner class compile, and what does a call return? A It compiles and returns 4 B It compiles and returns 0 C It does not compile. Inner classes cannot read private members D It does not compile. Inner classes cannot have methods 揭曉答案 M2. Does this compile? void run() { int n = 1; class Local { int value() { return n; } } n = 2; System.out.println(new Local().value()); } A It prints 1 B It prints 2 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 M3. What is the output? Pet pet = new Pet() { public String name() { return \"Cy\"; } }; System.out.println(pet.speak() + \" \" + pet.name()); Pet is the interface from Pets . A pet Cy B meow Cy C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 M4. Fly and Swim both declare default String move() { return \"go\"; } . Duck implements both and does not override move . Does Duck compile? A Yes, and move returns go B Yes, and the call is ambiguous at runtime C No. The class must override move D No. An interface cannot have a default method 揭曉答案 M5. sealed interface Shape permits Box {} and class Box implements Shape {} . Box is not final , sealed , or non-sealed . Does this compile? A Yes B Yes, because Box is in permits C No. A permitted class must say how it continues the seal D No. A sealed type cannot be an interface 揭曉答案 M6. Animal a = new Dog() , with the classes from Zoo . What is (a instanceof Dog d) ? d.speak() : a.kind ? A animal B woof C dog D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card An override matches the signature. The return may be covariant. Access stays the same or gets wider. Checked exceptions stay the same, get narrower, or disappear. static hides. private is not overridden. final cannot be overridden. @Override checks that an override is real. The reference type decides what compiles and which field is read. The object type decides which override runs. instanceof on null is false . A bad cast throws ClassCastException . If equals is true, the hash codes match. equals(null) is false . An interface static method is called on the interface. A default method is inherited. Two conflicting defaults must be overridden. A private interface method stays inside the interface. An abstract class cannot be constructed. A concrete subclass implements every abstract method. abstract is not final , and an abstract method is not private or static . A record accessor is the component name. A compact constructor assigns the parameters, and the fields are stored after that. A sealed switch is exhaustive when every permitted type has a case. A general pattern above a specific one does not compile. when guards a pattern. case null handles null. A static nested class has no outer instance. An inner class does. Local and anonymous classes see effectively final locals."}, {"href": "Week-04-glossary.html", "title": "Week 4 glossary — inheritance through pattern matching", "hay": "Week 4 glossary — inheritance through pattern matching Terms from Week-04-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — overriding Term Meaning extends A class has one superclass. The child constructor calls a parent constructor first. Override An instance method in the subclass replaces the parent method for objects of the subclass. Covariant return An override may return the parent’s return type or a subtype of it. Dog create() can override Animal create() . Override access The same access or wider. public cannot become package-private. Override exceptions A checked exception stays the same, becomes a subtype, or disappears. The child cannot throw a broader checked exception. @Override Asks the compiler to check that a real override exists. A different parameter list is an overload, so @Override on that method does not compile. Hidden static method A static method with the same signature in the subclass hides the parent method. The reference type selects it. It is not an override. private method Not visible to the subclass, so a child method of the same name is a new method. A parent method that calls the private one does not dispatch to the child. final method Cannot be redeclared in a subclass. abstract class Cannot be constructed with new . It may have fields, constructors, and ordinary methods. A concrete subclass implements every abstract method. super.speak() Calls the parent version from inside the child. Wednesday — reference type and object type Term Meaning Reference type The type written on the variable. It decides which names compile and which field is read. Object type The class after new . It decides which override runs. Field hiding A field in the subclass hides the parent field. It is not overridden. ((Dog) a).kind reads Dog ’s field. instanceof Tests the object type. null instanceof Dog is false and does not throw. Pattern instanceof if (a instanceof Dog d) both tests and assigns. Inside the block, d has type Dog . ClassCastException Thrown when a cast’s type is in the hierarchy but the object is not that type. (Dog) new Animal() throws it. Friday — equals , interfaces, enums Term Meaning equals contract Reflexive, symmetric, transitive, and consistent. x.equals(null) is false . hashCode contract If a.equals(b) is true, the hash codes match. Matching hash codes do not imply equals . Broken equals An equals that compares values while hashCode stays the identity hash from Object . A HashSet can then hold two objects that say they are equal. Default method An instance method in an interface with a body. Implementing classes inherit it and may override it. Interface static method Called on the interface name, as in Pet.home() . It is not inherited. DogPet.home() does not compile. Conflicting defaults Two interfaces with the same default signature. The class must override that method. Private interface method Has a body and is visible only inside that interface. Implementers do not inherit it. A static interface method cannot call a private instance method. Enum A fixed set of constants. The constructor is private. new Season(...) from outside does not compile. ordinal starts at 0. Saturday — records, sealed types, patterns Term Meaning Record A final class with one field per component, a canonical constructor, accessors named after the components, and generated equals , hashCode , and toString . Record accessor The component name. cents() , not getCents() . Compact constructor public Coin { ... } with no parameter list. You assign the component parameters. The fields are stored after that. Sealed type Lists its permitted subtypes with permits . Each direct subtype is final , sealed , or non-sealed . A record that implements a sealed interface is implicitly final . Exhaustive switch A switch that covers every remaining value. On a sealed type, one case per permitted type is enough, so default is optional. Type pattern case String s . It tests the type and assigns the variable. Record pattern case Point(int x, int y) . It takes the components out of a matching record. when A boolean guard on a pattern. case Point(int x, int y) when x > 0 . Dominated pattern A later case that can never run because an earlier case already covers it. It does not compile. Put the more specific pattern first. case null Handles a null selector. Without it, a null selector throws NullPointerException . Sunday — nested classes Term Meaning Static nested class Declared with static . No hidden outer instance. It can use the outer class’s private static members. Created with new Outer.Nested() . Inner class A member class without static . Each object has a hidden outer object. It can read the outer class’s private instance members. Outer.this is that outer object. Local class Declared inside a method. No access modifier. It can use effectively final local variables and parameters. Anonymous class An expression with no name. It extends one class or implements one interface. No constructor of its own. It can use effectively final locals. Effectively final A local variable that is assigned once and never assigned again. Local and anonymous classes, and lambdas in Week 7, may use only these locals."}, {"href": "Week-05-notes.html", "title": "Week 5 notes — exceptions and the date-time API", "hay": "Week 5 notes — exceptions and the date-time API 15 hours. Friday is a day off this week (see the 15-week plan for the calendar). This file follows Week 5 of the study plan. The gate is the exceptions and date-time questions at 70% (14 of the 20 questions in this file). Monday and Wednesday are 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Friday is a day off. Saturday is 6 hours. Sunday is 3 hours: the daylight-saving listing, 15 formatter predictions, and the questions. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 4 misses. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon try / catch / finally , multi-catch, checked versus unchecked Chapter 1, questions Q1–Q6 Wed Try-with-resources and a custom checked exception Chapter 2, Doors , Pay , questions Q1–Q4 Fri Off — Sat LocalDate , LocalTime , LocalDateTime , Period , Duration , Instant Chapter 3, Dates , questions Q1–Q5 Sun One daylight-saving example, 15 formatter predictions, then questions Dst , the 15 patterns, questions Q1–Q5, then score all 20 Same checklist as the study plan: From memory: checked versus unchecked versus Error , and what finally does when the catch block throws. A custom checked exception, and a try-with-resources class whose close messages print in reverse order of creation. LocalDate , LocalTime , LocalDateTime , Instant , Period , and Duration . Period is date-based. Duration is time-based. plusDays returns a new object. On a real daylight-saving date, show a local time that is skipped or repeated, and what ZonedDateTime does. Predict 15 DateTimeFormatter patterns, then run them. Exceptions and date-time questions at 70%. Predict each listing before you run it. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — try , catch , finally 必睇 第 11 章 Exceptions and Localization，pp. 614–636：由 “Understanding Exceptions” 到 “Adding a finally Block”。 唔使睇 “Automating Resource Management”（p. 637）係星期三。p. 646 起嘅格式同 locale 留星期日同第 8 週。Review Questions 唔使做。 Monday is this chapter and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know Throwable has two branches. Error means the JVM is in trouble ( OutOfMemoryError , StackOverflowError ). You do not catch Error in ordinary code, and you do not declare it. Exception splits again: Unchecked: RuntimeException and its subclasses, such as NullPointerException , IllegalArgumentException , ClassCastException , and ArithmeticException . A method may throw them without a throws clause. Checked: other Exception subclasses, such as IOException and FileNotFoundException . The caller catches them or declares them. Exception itself is checked. RuntimeException is not. catch clauses are tried in order. A later catch cannot use a parent of an earlier catch, or the earlier one would have taken everything. catch (FileNotFoundException | IOException e) does not compile, because the alternatives are related by inheritance. The parameter of a multi-catch is effectively final. finally runs after try and after catch , on the way out, including when the try returns. A System.exit call is the case that skips it. If finally throws, that exception replaces the one that was pending from try or catch . The pending one is not added as a suppressed exception. Suppressed exceptions belong to try-with-resources, which is Wednesday. This method prints catch finally-ran when finally does not throw, because finally runs and then the catch exception continues: try { throw new RuntimeException(\"try\"); } catch (RuntimeException e) { throw new RuntimeException(\"catch\"); } finally { System.out.print(\"finally-ran \"); } When finally itself throws new RuntimeException(\"finally\") , the caller sees the message finally , and getSuppressed() is empty. Monday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 1 questions Q1. Which type is checked? A IllegalArgumentException B IOException C NullPointerException D StackOverflowError 揭曉答案 Q2. What reaches the caller? try { throw new RuntimeException(\"try\"); } catch (RuntimeException e) { throw new RuntimeException(\"catch\"); } finally { throw new RuntimeException(\"finally\"); } A The message try , with catch suppressed B The message catch , with finally suppressed C The message finally , and getSuppressed() is empty D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. Does this compile? try { throw new java.io.FileNotFoundException(); } catch (java.io.FileNotFoundException | java.io.IOException e) { } A Yes B Yes, and e can be assigned a new exception C No. The two types are related by inheritance D No. FileNotFoundException is unchecked 揭曉答案 Q4. This method returns a String . The caller does not print the returned value. What does the method print? static String go() { String s = \"go\"; try { s = s + \" try\"; return s; } finally { s = s + \" finally\"; System.out.print(s); } } A go try B go try finally C go finally D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q5. Which catch order compiles? catch (Exception e) { } catch (RuntimeException e) { } A This order B The same catches swapped, with RuntimeException first C Both orders D Neither. RuntimeException cannot be caught 揭曉答案 Q6. A method calls Files.readString and does not catch IOException or declare it. What is the result? A It compiles. IOException is unchecked B It compiles. finally is implied C It does not compile D It compiles and throws Error if the file is missing 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Try-with-resources and a custom checked exception 必睇 第 11 章 “Automating Resource Management”，pp. 637–645。自訂 checked exception 在 “Throwing an Exception”（p. 618）；星期一未讀完就補呢一節。 唔使睇 p. 646 之後今日唔好睇。 Wednesday is this chapter, Doors , Pay , and questions Q1–Q4. What you must know A try-with-resources declares resources in parentheses. Each resource’s type implements AutoCloseable . The resources close automatically, after the body, in reverse order of creation. The first one created is the last one closed. If the body throws and close also throws, the body’s exception is the one that propagates. Each exception from close is added with addSuppressed . This is different from a finally that throws. The resource variable is effectively final. Assigning it inside the body does not compile. A custom checked exception extends Exception and not RuntimeException . Callers must catch it or declare it. Doors class Door implements AutoCloseable { private final String name; Door(String name) { this.name = name; System.out.print(name); } public void close() { System.out.print(name.toLowerCase()); } } public class Doors { public static void main(String[] args) { try (Door a = new Door(\"A\"); Door b = new Door(\"B\")) { System.out.print(\"-\"); } } } The run prints: AB-ba A and B are constructed, the body prints - , then B closes, then A closes. Pay class BadAmount extends Exception { BadAmount(String message) { super(message); } } public class Pay { static void charge(int cents) throws BadAmount { if (cents <= 0) { throw new BadAmount(\"cents\"); } } public static void main(String[] args) throws BadAmount { charge(50); System.out.println(\"charged\"); } } The run prints: charged Remove throws BadAmount from main and main does not compile. charge(-1) throws BadAmount before charged . Wednesday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 2 questions Q1. What does Doors print? A AB-ab B AB-ba C baAB- D AB-AB 揭曉答案 Q2. The body throws IllegalStateException(\"body\") and close throws IllegalStateException(\"close\") . What does the caller observe? A Message close , and body is suppressed B Message body , and close is suppressed C Message finally D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. Does this compile? Door d = new Door(\"A\"); try (d) { d = new Door(\"B\"); } A Yes, and the close order is ba B Yes, and only A is closed C No. The resource variable is effectively final D No. A resource must be Closeable , not AutoCloseable 揭曉答案 Q4. charge throws BadAmount . main calls charge(50) and does not catch or declare BadAmount . What is the result? A It compiles and prints charged B It compiles and throws BadAmount at runtime C It does not compile D It compiles because BadAmount extends RuntimeException 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Date and time 必睇 第 4 章 “Working with Dates and Times”，pp. 205–219：由 “Creating Dates and Times” 到 “Working with Instants”。 唔使睇 “Accounting for Daylight Saving Time”（p. 220）同第 11 章嘅 formatter 係星期日。locale（第 11 章 p. 651 起）留第 8 週。 Saturday is 6 hours: this chapter, Dates , and questions Q1–Q5. What you must know These types are immutable. A plus method returns a new object. The original variable does not change until you assign the result. Type What it stores LocalDate A date, with no time and no zone LocalTime A time, with no date and no zone LocalDateTime A date and a time, with no zone Instant A point on the UTC timeline Period Years, months, and days. Date-based Duration Hours, minutes, seconds, and nanos. Time-based ZonedDateTime A date, a time, and a zone. Sunday uses it Period.of(1, 2, 3) is one year, two months, and three days. Period.between(start, end) counts from the start date to the end date. Duration.ofHours(2).plusMinutes(30) prints PT2H30M . LocalDate.plus(Duration.ofDays(1)) throws UnsupportedTemporalTypeException , because a date has no time fields for a Duration to change. LocalDate.plus(Period.ofDays(1)) works. LocalDateTime can take a Period or a Duration . Instant takes a Duration . Adding a Period to an Instant does not fit: an instant has no calendar fields. Dates import java.time.*; public class Dates { public static void main(String[] args) { LocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 21); d.plusDays(1); System.out.println(d); System.out.println(d.plusDays(1)); LocalDate later = d.plus(Period.ofDays(2)); System.out.println(later); System.out.println(Period.between(d, later)); System.out.println(Duration.ofHours(2).plusMinutes(30)); } } The run prints: 2026-12-21 2026-12-22 2026-12-23 P2D PT2H30M The first line is still 21 December. plusDays returned a new date and the code ignored it. Saturday’s questions are Q1–Q5. Chapter 3 questions Q1. LocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 21); d.plusDays(1); What is d ? A 2026-12-21 B 2026-12-22 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q2. What is Period.between(LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 21), LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 23)) ? A PT48H B P2D C P0Y2M D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. What happens on LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 21).plus(Duration.ofDays(1)) ? A It returns 2026-12-22 B It returns a LocalDateTime C It throws UnsupportedTemporalTypeException D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q4. Which addition fits the type? A Instant.now().plus(Period.ofDays(1)) B LocalDate.now().plus(Duration.ofHours(1)) C LocalTime.now().plus(Duration.ofHours(1)) D LocalDate.now().plus(Duration.ofDays(1)) 揭曉答案 Q5. What does Duration.ofHours(2).plusMinutes(30) print? A 2:30 B PT2H30M C P2H30M D 150 揭曉答案 Sunday — daylight saving, 15 patterns, then the score 必睇 第 4 章 “Accounting for Daylight Saving Time”，p. 220。第 11 章 “Formatting Dates and Times” 同 “Customizing the Date/Time Format”，pp. 647–650。15 條 pattern 用呢兩節。 唔使睇 p. 651 起嘅 locale、貨幣、resource bundle 留第 8 週。 Sunday is 3 hours. The first 20 minutes are Saturday’s misses. Dst 8 March 2026 is the New York spring-forward. Local time 02:30 does not exist. 1 November 2026 is the fall-back. Local time 01:30 happens twice. import java.time.*; public class Dst { public static void main(String[] args) { ZoneId ny = ZoneId.of(\"America/New_York\"); LocalDateTime gap = LocalDateTime.of(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30); System.out.println(ZonedDateTime.of(gap, ny)); LocalDateTime overlap = LocalDateTime.of(2026, 11, 1, 1, 30); System.out.println(ZonedDateTime.of(overlap, ny)); System.out.println(ZonedDateTime.ofLocal(overlap, ny, ZoneOffset.ofHours(-5))); } } The run prints: 2026-03-08T03:30-04:00[America/New_York] 2026-11-01T01:30-04:00[America/New_York] 2026-11-01T01:30-05:00[America/New_York] ZonedDateTime.of moves the missing 02:30 forward to 03:30. On the overlap, of picks the earlier offset, which is still -04:00 . ofLocal with the preferred offset -05:00 picks the later, standard-time occurrence. Fifteen patterns Use LocalDateTime.of(2026, 12, 21, 15, 4, 5) and Locale.US . That date is a Monday. Write the fifteen results before you run them. Then run a loop of DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(pattern, Locale.US) , and use DateTimeFormatter.ISO_LOCAL_DATE for the last one. # Pattern Result 1 yyyy-MM-dd 2026-12-21 2 dd/MM/yyyy 21/12/2026 3 MMM d, yyyy Dec 21, 2026 4 MMMM December 5 EEE Mon 6 HH:mm:ss 15:04:05 7 h:mm a 3:04 PM 8 yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm 2026-12-21T15:04 9 D 355 10 uuuu 2026 11 q 4 12 e 2 13 a PM 14 ss 05 15 ISO_LOCAL_DATE 2026-12-21 D is the day of the year. q is the quarter. e is the numeric day of the week for this locale, and Monday is 2 because the US week starts on Sunday. A single M would print 12 without a leading zero. MM prints 12 . MMM prints Dec . Sunday questions Q1. What does ZonedDateTime.of(LocalDateTime.of(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30), ZoneId.of(\"America/New_York\")) use as the local time? A 02:30 B 03:30 C 01:30 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. On 1 November 2026 in America/New_York , which offset does ZonedDateTime.of choose for local time 01:30? A -04:00 B -05:00 C Z D The call throws DateTimeException 揭曉答案 Q3. What is pattern yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm for 21 Dec 2026 at 15:04:05? A 2026-12-21 15:04:05 B 2026-12-21T15:04 C 21/12/2026T3:04 PM D The pattern does not compile 揭曉答案 Q4. What does pattern D print for 21 Dec 2026? A 21 B 12 C 355 D Monday 揭曉答案 Q5. LocalDate and LocalDateTime are created with of . You call plusDays(1) and do not assign the result. Which statement is true? A Both variables move forward one day B Both variables stay on the original date C Only LocalDate stays. LocalDateTime changes D The calls do not compile 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card Checked exceptions must be caught or declared. RuntimeException is unchecked. Error is a separate branch. catch goes from the specific type to the general type. Multi-catch alternatives cannot be related by inheritance. finally runs on the way out. If finally throws, that exception replaces the pending one. Try-with-resources closes in reverse order of creation. An exception from close is suppressed when the body also threw. A custom checked exception extends Exception , not RuntimeException . Period is date-based ( P2D ). Duration is time-based ( PT2H30M ). plus returns a new object. LocalDate accepts a Period and rejects a Duration . A missing local time in a zone is moved. A repeated local time has two offsets. yyyy is the year, MM the month, dd the day, HH the hour 0–23, D the day of the year."}, {"href": "Week-05-glossary.html", "title": "Week 5 glossary — exceptions and the date-time API", "hay": "Week 5 glossary — exceptions and the date-time API Terms from Week-05-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — try , catch , finally Term Meaning Throwable The parent of Error and Exception . Error A serious JVM failure, such as OutOfMemoryError or StackOverflowError . Ordinary code does not catch it and does not declare it. Checked exception An Exception that is not a RuntimeException , such as IOException . The caller catches it or declares it. Exception itself is checked. Unchecked exception RuntimeException and its subclasses, such as NullPointerException , IllegalArgumentException , ClassCastException , and ArithmeticException . No throws clause is required. catch order Specific type first, then the parent type. A later catch of a child of an earlier catch does not compile. Multi-catch catch (A \\| B e) . The alternatives cannot be related by inheritance. e is effectively final. finally Runs on the way out of try and catch , including when the try returns. If finally throws, that exception replaces the pending one. The pending one is not added as a suppressed exception. Wednesday — resources and custom exceptions Term Meaning Try-with-resources A try that declares resources in parentheses. Each type implements AutoCloseable . Close order After the body, resources close in reverse order of creation. The first one created is the last one closed. Suppressed exception When the body throws and close also throws, the body’s exception propagates and the close exception is added with addSuppressed . Effectively final resource A resource variable cannot be reassigned, including one declared outside and named in the parentheses. Custom checked exception A class that extends Exception and not RuntimeException . Callers must catch it or declare it. Saturday — date and time Term Meaning LocalDate A date, with no time and no zone. LocalTime A time, with no date and no zone. LocalDateTime A date and a time, with no zone. Instant A point on the UTC timeline. Period A date-based amount: years, months, and days. Printed as P2D . Duration A time-based amount: hours, minutes, seconds, and nanos. Printed as PT2H30M . Immutable date plusDays and the other plus methods return a new object. The original variable does not change until you assign the result. Period.between The period from the start date to the end date. Date plus Duration LocalDate.plus(Duration) throws UnsupportedTemporalTypeException . A date has no time fields. LocalTime accepts a Duration . An Instant accepts a Duration and rejects a Period . Sunday — zones and formatters Term Meaning ZonedDateTime A date, a time, and a zone. Gap A local time that does not exist, such as 02:30 in America/New_York on 8 March 2026. ZonedDateTime.of moves it forward. That call produces 2026-03-08T03:30-04:00 . Overlap A local time that happens twice, such as 01:30 in New York on 1 November 2026. ZonedDateTime.of picks the earlier offset, -04:00 . ofLocal can pick -05:00 when you pass that offset. DateTimeFormatter Turns a date or time into text from a pattern, or parses text. Use Locale.US when the pattern includes words. yyyy The year. uuuu is also the year. MM The month, with a leading zero. MMM is the short name ( Dec ). MMMM is the full name ( December ). dd The day of the month. HH The hour from 0 to 23. h is the hour on a 12-hour clock. a is the AM/PM marker. D The day of the year. 21 December 2026 is 355 . q The quarter. December is 4 . e The numeric day of the week for the locale. In Locale.US the week starts on Sunday, so Monday is 2 . EEE The short weekday name. 21 December 2026 is Mon . Quoted pattern text Characters in single quotes are literals. yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm keeps the T . ISO_LOCAL_DATE The predefined formatter yyyy-MM-dd ."}, {"href": "Week-06-notes.html", "title": "Week 6 notes — collections and generics", "hay": "Week 6 notes — collections and generics 15 hours. Friday is a day off this week (see the 15-week plan for the calendar). This file follows Week 6 of the study plan. The gate is the collections and generics questions at 70% (14 of the 20 questions in this file). Monday and Wednesday are 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Friday is a day off. Saturday is 6 hours. Sunday is 3 hours: generics, then the questions, then the score. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 5 misses. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon List , and the remove overload trap Chapter 1, Removes , questions Q1–Q4 Wed Set , Map , null rules, and order Chapter 2, SameData , questions Q1–Q4 Fri Off — Sat Deque , sequenced methods, Comparable , and Comparator Chapter 3, Lines , Names , questions Q1–Q6 Sun Generics, then the questions Chapter 4, questions Q1–Q6, then score all 20 Same checklist as the study plan: Show list.remove(1) versus list.remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) . The same data in a List , a HashSet , a TreeSet , and a HashMap . Write down what is ordered and what allows null. Use a Deque as a stack and as a queue. Call getFirst , getLast , addFirst , addLast , and reversed . Sort with Comparable and with Comparator.comparing followed by thenComparing . For ? extends and for ? super , say whether you can add and whether you can get. One example of each. Collections and generics questions at 70%. Predict each listing before you run it. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — List and remove 必睇 第 9 章 Collections and Generics，pp. 482–495：“Using Common Collection APIs” 同 “Using the List Interface”。 唔使睇 “Working with Generics”（p. 526）留星期日。Review Questions 唔使做。 Monday is this chapter, Removes , and questions Q1–Q4. What you must know List keeps insertion order and allows duplicates. list.get(i) and list.remove(i) use an index when the argument is an int . remove is overloaded. remove(int) deletes by index. remove(Object) deletes the first element that equals the argument. For a List<Integer> , the literal 1 is an int , so remove(1) deletes index 1. remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) deletes the value 1. List.of returns an immutable list. It rejects null and it rejects add or remove . Arrays.asList is fixed-size and backed by the array: set works, add and remove throw UnsupportedOperationException . Removes import java.util.*; public class Removes { public static void main(String[] args) { List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3)); list.remove(1); System.out.println(list); list.remove(Integer.valueOf(1)); System.out.println(list); } } The run prints: [1, 3] [3] remove(1) deleted the element at index 1, which was 2 . The list was [1, 3] . remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) then deleted the value 1 . Monday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 1 questions Q1. What is the list after remove(1) in Removes , before the second remove? A [1, 2, 3] B [2, 3] C [1, 3] D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3)); What does list.remove(Integer.valueOf(2)) leave? A [1, 3] B [1, 2] C [1, 2, 3] D It throws IndexOutOfBoundsException 揭曉答案 Q3. What is the result of List.of(1, 2).add(3) ? A The list becomes [1, 2, 3] B The code does not compile C An UnsupportedOperationException is thrown D An IndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q4. String[] names = {\"Ada\"}; List<String> list = Arrays.asList(names); Which call succeeds? A list.add(\"Bea\") B list.set(0, \"Bea\") C list.remove(0) D None of them 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Set , Map , null, and order 必睇 第 9 章 “Using the Set Interface”，pp. 496–497。“Using the Map Interface”，pp. 502–508。 唔使睇 Queue / Deque（pp. 498–501）係星期六。 Wednesday is this chapter, SameData , and questions Q1–Q4. What you must know Write this table from memory before you run SameData . Type Order Null ArrayList Insertion order, duplicates allowed Null elements allowed HashSet No order you can rely on One null element TreeSet Sorted No null when it uses natural order HashMap No key order you can rely on One null key, and null values TreeMap Keys sorted No null key when it uses natural order HashSet and HashMap use hashCode and equals . TreeSet and TreeMap use Comparable or a Comparator . A TreeSet of String prints in alphabetical order. Map keys are unique by equals . Putting the same key again replaces the value. get on a missing key returns null . A null value that was stored also returns null , so containsKey is the test for a missing key. SameData import java.util.*; public class SameData { public static void main(String[] args) { List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"a\")); Set<String> hash = new HashSet<>(list); Set<String> tree = new TreeSet<>(list); Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>(); map.put(\"b\", 1); map.put(\"a\", 2); map.put(\"c\", 3); map.put(\"a\", 9); System.out.println(list); System.out.println(hash.size()); System.out.println(tree); System.out.println(map.get(\"a\")); } } The run prints the list [b, a, c, a] , the hash size 3 , the tree [a, b, c] , and the map value 9 . The hash set’s own order is not part of the result. The size is. The second put of \"a\" replaced 2 with 9 . Wednesday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 2 questions Q1. What does SameData print for the TreeSet ? A [b, a, c, a] B [a, b, c] C [b, a, c] D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. What is map.get(\"a\") after the two put calls for \"a\" ? A 2 B 9 C null D An exception is thrown 揭曉答案 Q3. Does new TreeSet<String>().add(null) succeed? A Yes. A set may hold one null B Yes. TreeSet sorts null first C No. Natural order has no place for null, and the call throws NullPointerException D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q4. A HashMap already contains the key null . What is the result of a second put(null, 1) ? A It throws NullPointerException B It stores a second null key C It replaces the value for the one null key D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Deque , sequenced collections, and sorting 必睇 第 9 章 pp. 498–501（Queue 同 Deque）、pp. 509–525（sorting、sequenced collections、“Reviewing Collection Types”）。 唔使睇 generics 仍然留星期日。 Saturday is 6 hours: this chapter, Lines , Names , and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know Deque is a double-ended queue. As a stack, push and pop use the front. As a queue, offer adds at the back and poll removes from the front. ArrayDeque does not allow null elements. Java 21 sequenced collections add addFirst , addLast , getFirst , getLast , removeFirst , removeLast , and reversed . reversed returns a reverse-ordered view. List , Deque , SortedSet , and LinkedHashSet are sequenced. HashSet is not. Comparable.compareTo defines a class’s natural order. The sign is negative, zero, or positive, in the same style as Week 2’s Arrays.compare . Comparator.comparing builds an order from a method. thenComparing breaks ties with a second order. Sort a List with list.sort(comparator) . Lines import java.util.*; public class Lines { public static void main(String[] args) { Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>(); stack.push(\"a\"); stack.push(\"b\"); System.out.print(stack.pop()); System.out.print(stack.pop()); Deque<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>(); queue.offer(\"a\"); queue.offer(\"b\"); System.out.print(queue.poll()); System.out.print(queue.poll()); Deque<String> seq = new ArrayDeque<>(); seq.addLast(\"a\"); seq.addFirst(\"b\"); System.out.print(seq.getFirst()); System.out.print(seq.getLast()); System.out.println(seq.reversed()); } } The run prints: baabba[a, b] Read it as ba , then ab , then ba , then [a, b] . The stack pops b then a . The queue polls a then b . After addLast(\"a\") and addFirst(\"b\") , the first element is b and the last is a . The reversed view prints [a, b] . Names import java.util.*; public class Names { public static void main(String[] args) { List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"Ada\", \"Bea\", \"Cy\")); names.sort(Comparator.comparing(String::length).thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder())); System.out.println(names); } } The run prints: [Cy, Ada, Bea] Cy has length 2, so it comes first. Ada and Bea both have length 3, so natural alphabetical order puts Ada before Bea . Saturday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 3 questions Q1. A stack is pushed \"a\" then \"b\" . What does the first pop return? A a B b C null D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q2. A queue is offered \"a\" then \"b\" . What does the first poll return? A a B b C null D It throws NoSuchElementException 揭曉答案 Q3. addLast(\"a\") then addFirst(\"b\") . What are getFirst and getLast ? A a then b B b then a C a then a D The code does not compile on a Deque 揭曉答案 Q4. What does seq.reversed() print after those two adds? A [b, a] B [a, b] C [a] D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q5. What does Names print? A [Ada, Bea, Cy] B [Cy, Bea, Ada] C [Cy, Ada, Bea] D [Ada, Cy, Bea] 揭曉答案 Q6. compareTo returns a negative number. Where does this object belong relative to the argument? A After the argument B Before the argument C At the same position, and equals must be true D The sort throws IllegalArgumentException 揭曉答案 Chapter 4 — Generics 必睇 “Working with Generics”，pp. 526–542，包括 Summary 同 Exam Essentials。 唔使睇 Review Questions（p. 543 起）唔使做。 Sunday is 3 hours. The first 20 minutes are Saturday’s misses. Then this chapter, questions Q1–Q6, and the score. What you must know A generic class stores a type parameter, such as List<String> . The compiler checks the type. At runtime the parameter is erased, so List<String> and List<Integer> are both just List . You cannot overload two methods that erase to the same signature. List<? extends Number> is a producer. You can get a Number . You cannot add an Integer or a Double , because the list might be a List<Integer> or a List<Double> . null is the one reference you may add. List<? super Integer> is a consumer. You can add an Integer . get returns Object , because the list might be a List<Integer> , a List<Number> , or a List<Object> . Remember it as: extends lets you get, super lets you add. An unbounded ? lets you get Object and lets you add only null . List<? extends Number> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>(); Number n = nums.size() == 0 ? null : nums.get(0); List<? super Integer> sink = new ArrayList<Number>(); sink.add(1); Object got = sink.get(0); got is the Integer 1 , seen through an Object variable. nums.add(1) does not compile. Chapter 4 questions Q1. List<? extends Number> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>(); Does nums.add(1) compile? A Yes B Yes, but only because 1 is an Integer C No D It compiles and throws ClassCastException 揭曉答案 Q2. List<? super Integer> sink = new ArrayList<Number>(); What is the compile-time type of sink.get(0) after sink.add(1) ? A Integer B Number C Object D The get does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. Does this compile? void show(List<String> a) {} void show(List<Integer> a) {} A Yes B Yes, because the type arguments differ C No. Erasure gives both methods the same signature D No. A method cannot take a List 揭曉答案 Q4. What can you add to a List<?> besides leaving it unchanged? A Any Object B Only null C Only String D Nothing, including null 揭曉答案 Q5. Which assignment compiles? List<String> words = new ArrayList<>(); A List<Object> objs = words; B List<? extends CharSequence> objs = words; C List<CharSequence> objs = words; D List<? super Object> objs = words; 揭曉答案 Q6. TreeSet<String> natural order versus HashSet . Which statement is true? A Both print elements in insertion order B TreeSet is sorted. HashSet has no order you can rely on C Both reject duplicate equals matches and both allow one null D HashSet rejects null and TreeSet allows one 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card remove(int) deletes an index. remove(Object) deletes a value. On List<Integer> , remove(1) is the index. List.of rejects null and rejects modification. Arrays.asList allows set and refuses add and remove . HashSet and HashMap have no reliable order. TreeSet and TreeMap are sorted. One null key is legal in HashMap and illegal in a naturally ordered TreeMap . A stack pops the last push. A queue polls the first offer. getFirst and getLast read the ends. reversed is a reverse view. comparing then thenComparing sorts by the first key and breaks ties with the second. A negative compareTo sorts first. ? extends can get and cannot add. ? super can add and get returns Object . Overloads that erase to the same signature do not compile."}, {"href": "Week-06-glossary.html", "title": "Week 6 glossary — collections and generics", "hay": "Week 6 glossary — collections and generics Terms from Week-06-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — List Term Meaning List An ordered collection that allows duplicates. get and the int form of remove use an index. remove(int) Deletes by index. On a List<Integer> , remove(1) deletes index 1. remove(Object) Deletes the first element that equals the argument. remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) deletes the value 1. List.of An immutable list. It rejects null . add and remove throw UnsupportedOperationException . Arrays.asList A fixed-size list backed by the array. set works. add and remove throw UnsupportedOperationException . Wednesday — Set and Map Term Meaning HashSet A set with no order you can rely on. Allows one null . Uses hashCode and equals . TreeSet A sorted set. Natural order rejects null with NullPointerException . Uses Comparable or a Comparator . HashMap A map with no key order you can rely on. One null key, and null values. Putting the same key again replaces the value. TreeMap A map with sorted keys. Natural order rejects a null key. get Returns the value, or null when the key is missing. A stored null value also returns null . containsKey Tells a missing key from a key whose stored value is null . Saturday — Deque , sequenced collections, sorting Term Meaning Deque A double-ended queue. ArrayDeque does not allow null elements. Stack methods push and pop use the front. The first pop returns the last push . pop on an empty deque throws NoSuchElementException . Queue methods offer adds at the back. poll removes from the front. poll on an empty deque returns null . Sequenced collection Has addFirst , addLast , getFirst , getLast , removeFirst , removeLast , and reversed . List , Deque , SortedSet , and LinkedHashSet are sequenced. HashSet is not. reversed A reverse-ordered view of the same collection, not a new copy of the elements. Comparable A class’s natural order, through compareTo . A negative result sorts this object before the argument. Zero does not by itself prove equals . Comparator An order supplied from outside the class. comparing Builds a comparator from a method, such as String::length . thenComparing A second comparator used only when the first comparison is a tie. Sunday — generics Term Meaning Generic type A type with a parameter, such as List<String> . The compiler checks it. At runtime the parameter is erased. Erasure List<String> and List<Integer> are both List at runtime. Two methods that erase to the same signature do not compile. Invariant List<String> is not a List<Object> and not a List<CharSequence> . ? extends A producer. get returns the upper bound. You cannot add an element, except null , because the list might be a more specific type. ? super A consumer. You can add the lower bound. get returns Object . Unbounded ? get returns Object . The only reference you may add is null ."}, {"href": "Week-07-notes.html", "title": "Week 7 notes — lambdas and streams", "hay": "Week 7 notes — lambdas and streams 25 hours. Weekend days are 8 hours. This file follows Week 7 of the study plan. Calendar dates are only in the 15-week plan . The gate is the 30 stream questions at 70% (21 of 30). Under 60% (under 18) means the first days of the mock month are streams, coded, before any other objective. Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Saturday and Sunday are 8 hours each. The extra two hours are questions. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 6 misses. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon Lambda syntax and effectively final variables Chapter 1, the eight rewrites, questions Q1–Q6 Wed The six functional interfaces and method references Chapter 2, the 15 names, questions Q7–Q12 Fri Optional , including orElse versus orElseGet Chapter 3, Opts , questions Q13–Q18 Sat (8 h) Pipelines, on paper first Chapter 4, the 12 pipelines, questions Q19–Q24 Sun (8 h) groupingBy , primitive streams, 30 questions Chapter 5, questions Q25–Q30, then score all 30 Same checklist as the study plan: Rewrite eight anonymous classes as lambdas, then as method references: static, bound, unbound, and constructor. Name the functional interface from a snippet, 15 out of 15. Include a primitive form such as IntPredicate or ToIntFunction . Optional map , flatMap , orElse , and orElseGet . Show that orElse evaluates its argument even when a value is present. On paper, 12 pipelines using filter , map , flatMap , sorted , distinct , limit , reduce , and collect . Then type them. groupingBy into a map of lists, and into a map of counts. partitioningBy into true and false. IntStream.average returns OptionalDouble . Reuse a consumed stream and read the exception. 30 stream questions at 70%. Under 60%: the first days of the mock month are streams, coded, before any other objective. Predict each listing before you run it. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Chapter 1 — Lambda syntax 必睇 第 8 章 pp. 440–444（“Writing Simple Lambdas”）同 pp. 465–469（“Working with Variables in Lambdas”）。第 5 章 “Effectively Final Variables”，p. 244。 唔使睇 functional interfaces 同 method references 係星期三。Review Questions 唔使做。 Monday is this chapter, eight rewrites, and questions Q1–Q6. What you must know A lambda is a method body with no name. The target type is a functional interface: one abstract method. var is allowed for a parameter only inside parentheses, and if one parameter uses var then every parameter does. s -> s.isEmpty() (s) -> s.isEmpty() (String s) -> s.isEmpty() (var s) -> s.isEmpty() A body with braces uses return when the abstract method returns a value. A body without braces returns the expression and does not use return or a semicolon. A lambda may use a local variable only when that variable is final or effectively final. One assignment, and no assignment after that, makes it effectively final. A field does not have this limit. The lambda reads the field when it runs. Eight rewrites Type each anonymous class, then replace it with the lambda, then with the method reference. Two of each kind is enough for the checklist. Static: Function<String, Integer> a = new Function<>() { public Integer apply(String s) { return Integer.parseInt(s); } }; Function<String, Integer> b = s -> Integer.parseInt(s); Function<String, Integer> c = Integer::parseInt; Function<String, String> a = s -> s.trim(); Function<String, String> c = String::trim; String::trim is an unbound instance method. The instance arrives as the argument. Integer::parseInt is static. The argument is the parameter. Bound. The instance is already chosen: String word = \"hi\"; Supplier<Integer> a = new Supplier<>() { public Integer get() { return word.length(); } }; Supplier<Integer> b = () -> word.length(); Supplier<Integer> c = word::length; Unbound again, with the instance as the first argument: Function<String, Integer> len = String::length; BiFunction<String, String, String> cat = String::concat; Constructor: Supplier<List<String>> lists = ArrayList::new; Function<String, StringBuilder> builders = StringBuilder::new; ArrayList::new matches get() with the empty constructor. StringBuilder::new matches apply(String) with new StringBuilder(String) . Monday’s questions are Q1–Q6. Chapter 1 questions Q1. Does this compile? int n = 1; Runnable r = () -> System.out.println(n); n = 2; A Yes, and r.run() prints 2 B Yes, and r.run() prints 1 C No D It compiles and throws when r.run() is called 揭曉答案 Q2. Which lambda assigns to Supplier<String> ? A s -> \"ok\" B () -> \"ok\" C (String s) -> s D () -> {} 揭曉答案 Q3. Does this compile as a Function<String, Integer> ? s -> { s.length(); } A Yes B Yes, and the result is the length C No. A brace body that produces a value needs return D No. A lambda cannot use braces 揭曉答案 Q4. Does (var s) -> s.length() compile as a Function<String, Integer> ? A Yes B No. var is illegal in a lambda C No. var cannot sit inside parentheses D No. length returns int , not Integer 揭曉答案 Q5. String word = \"hi\"; Supplier<Integer> len = word::length; What does len.get() return? A 0 B 2 C \"hi\" D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q6. What does Function<String, Integer> f = Integer::parseInt; f.apply(\"10\") return? A \"10\" B 10 C 2 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Functional interfaces 必睇 第 8 章 pp. 445–464：“Coding Functional Interfaces”、“Using Method References”、“Working with Built-in Functional Interfaces”。 唔使睇 Optional 在第 10 章，星期五先睇。 Wednesday is this chapter, 15 names, and questions Q7–Q12. What you must know Interface Abstract method Supplier<T> T get() Consumer<T> void accept(T) Predicate<T> boolean test(T) Function<T, R> R apply(T) UnaryOperator<T> T apply(T) BinaryOperator<T> T apply(T, T) IntSupplier int getAsInt() IntConsumer void accept(int) IntPredicate boolean test(int) IntFunction<R> R apply(int) ToIntFunction<T> int applyAsInt(T) UnaryOperator<T> is a Function<T, T> . Use the primitive interface when the snippet takes or returns a primitive and the target type says so. Boxing still lets Function<String, Integer> accept String::length . Name these 15 Write the interface, including type arguments, before you look at the key. # Snippet 1 () -> \"a\" 2 s -> s.isEmpty() for a String argument and a boolean result 3 s -> System.out.println(s) for a String 4 s -> s.trim() for String in and String out 5 (a, b) -> a + b for two Integer s 6 () -> 1 as a primitive int 7 n -> n > 0 for a primitive int 8 s -> s.length() as a primitive int result from a String 9 n -> \"n\" + n from a primitive int to a String 10 n -> System.out.println(n) for a primitive int 11 (s, n) -> s.substring(n) for String and int 12 () -> {} 13 x -> x for one String 14 list -> list.add(\"a\") for a List<String> , result ignored 15 () -> new ArrayList<String>() The names are: 1 Supplier<String> . 2 Predicate<String> . 3 Consumer<String> . 4 UnaryOperator<String> . 5 BinaryOperator<Integer> . 6 IntSupplier . 7 IntPredicate . 8 ToIntFunction<String> . 9 IntFunction<String> . 10 IntConsumer . 11 BiFunction<String, Integer, String> . 12 Runnable . 13 UnaryOperator<String> . 14 Consumer<List<String>> . 15 Supplier<List<String>> . 15 of 15 is the checklist item. A miss here is a notebook line even if the later questions go well. Chapter 2 questions Q7. Predicate<String> p = String::isEmpty; What is p.test(\"\") ? A true B false C \"\" D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q8. What is the target of n -> n + 1 when n is an Integer and the result is an Integer ? A Predicate<Integer> B Consumer<Integer> C UnaryOperator<Integer> D IntSupplier 揭曉答案 Q9. ToIntFunction<String> f = String::length; What is f.applyAsInt(\"java\") ? A 4 B \"java\" C Integer.valueOf(4) as the only legal return D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q10. Supplier<List<String>> lists = ArrayList::new; What is lists.get().size() ? A null B 0 C The code does not compile D An exception is thrown at runtime 揭曉答案 Q11. Does IntPredicate p = n -> n > 0; p.test(1) compile and return true ? A It does not compile. IntPredicate has no test B It compiles and returns true C It compiles and returns false D It compiles and throws, because 1 is boxed 揭曉答案 Q12. Which reference is bound? A String::length B Integer::parseInt C word::length , where word is a String variable D ArrayList::new 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Optional 必睇 第 10 章 “Returning an Optional”，pp. 554–557。同後面 “Chaining Optionals”，p. 587。 唔使睇 中間嘅 stream pipeline 留星期六。唔好順住讀完整章。 Friday is this chapter, Opts , and questions Q13–Q18. What you must know Optional.of(value) rejects null. Optional.ofNullable(value) turns null into an empty optional. Optional.empty() is empty. map applies a function when a value is present and wraps the result. On an empty optional, map stays empty. flatMap is the same idea when the function already returns an Optional . Use flatMap so you do not end up with Optional<Optional<T>> . orElse(other) always evaluates other , then returns the stored value if there is one. orElseGet(supplier) calls the supplier only when the optional is empty. orElseThrow with no supplier throws NoSuchElementException when the optional is empty. Opts import java.util.*; public class Opts { static int calls; static String mark() { calls++; return \"x\"; } public static void main(String[] args) { Optional.of(\"a\").orElse(mark()); System.out.print(calls); Optional.of(\"a\").orElseGet(Opts::mark); System.out.println(calls); System.out.println(Optional.<String>empty().map(String::length)); System.out.println(Optional.of(\"hi\").flatMap(s -> Optional.of(s.length()))); } } The run prints: 11 Optional.empty Optional[2] orElse called mark even though \"a\" was present, so calls became 1. orElseGet did not call mark , so calls stayed 1. The two digits are stuck together: 11 . Friday’s questions are Q13–Q18. Chapter 3 questions Q13. Optional.of(\"a\").orElse(mark()) calls mark , and mark increments a counter that started at 0. What is the counter afterward? A 0 B 1 C The code does not compile D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q14. After that call, Optional.of(\"a\").orElseGet(Opts::mark) runs. What is the counter if it was already 1 ? A 1 B 2 C 0 D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q15. What is Optional.<String>empty().map(String::length) ? A 0 B Optional.empty C Optional[0] D A NullPointerException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q16. What does Optional.of(\"hi\").flatMap(s -> Optional.of(s.length())) print? A 2 B Optional[2] C Optional[Optional[2]] D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q17. What is the result of Optional.of(null) ? A An empty optional B Optional[null] C A NullPointerException is thrown D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q18. What does Optional.empty().orElseThrow() throw? A NullPointerException B IllegalStateException C NoSuchElementException D Nothing. It returns null 揭曉答案 Chapter 4 — Pipelines 必睇 第 10 章 “Using Streams”，pp. 558–578。 唔使睇 primitive streams（p. 579 起）同 “Collecting Results”（p. 590）留星期日。 Saturday is 8 hours. Write the result of each pipeline on paper, then type it. Questions Q19–Q24 are after the 12 lines. A pipeline is lazy. Intermediate operations ( filter , map , flatMap , sorted , distinct , limit , skip , peek ) do nothing until a terminal operation runs ( toList , reduce , count , collect , forEach , findFirst , anyMatch , allMatch , noneMatch ). A stream can be consumed once. Stream.toList() returns a list that refuses add . Twelve pipelines # Pipeline Result 1 List.of(1, 2, 2, 3).stream().filter(x -> x > 1).distinct().map(x -> x * 10).toList() [20, 30] 2 Stream.of(List.of(1, 2), List.of(3)).flatMap(List::stream).toList() [1, 2, 3] 3 Stream.of(1, 2, 3).reduce(0, Integer::sum) 6 4 Stream.of(3, 1, 2).sorted().limit(2).toList() [1, 2] 5 Stream.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"a\").distinct().toList() [a, b] 6 Stream.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\").skip(1).limit(1).toList() [b] 7 Stream.of(\"hi\", \"a\").map(String::length).toList() [2, 1] 8 Stream.of(1, 2, 3).anyMatch(n -> n > 2) true 9 Stream.of(1, 2).allMatch(n -> n > 0) true 10 Stream.of(1, 2).noneMatch(n -> n < 0) true 11 Stream.of(\"a\", \"a\").distinct().count() 1 12 Stream.of(\"a\", \"b\").collect(Collectors.joining(\",\")) a,b Lines 1, 2, and 3 were run on JDK 21. Run the other nine yourself and keep any miss. Chapter 4 questions Q19. What is pipeline 1? A [20, 20, 30] B [20, 30] C [10, 20, 30] D [2, 3] 揭曉答案 Q20. What is pipeline 2? A [[1, 2], [3]] B [1, 2, 3] C [1, 2] D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q21. What is pipeline 4? A [3, 1] B [1, 2] C [1, 2, 3] D [2, 3] 揭曉答案 Q22. A stream is built and peek prints each element. No terminal operation is called. What prints? A Every element B Nothing C The first element D An IllegalStateException is thrown 揭曉答案 Q23. Stream.of(\"a\").count() has already run. A second count on that same stream does what? A It returns 1 B It returns 0 C It throws IllegalStateException D It throws NullPointerException 揭曉答案 Q24. What does Stream.of(1, 2, 3).filter(n -> n > 1).toList().add(9) do? A The list becomes [2, 3, 9] B It throws UnsupportedOperationException C It throws IllegalStateException D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Chapter 5 — groupingBy and primitive streams 必睇 第 10 章 pp. 579–586（primitive streams）同 pp. 590–603（“Collecting Results”、Summary、Exam Essentials）。第 13 章 “Working with Parallel Streams”，pp. 786–794。 略讀 p. 587：“Linking Streams to the Underlying Data”。“Chaining Optionals” 星期五讀過就跳過。“Using a Spliterator”（p. 599）知道個名就得，唔使記 API。 唔使睇 第 13 章其餘 threads 留第 8 週。Review Questions 唔使做。 Sunday is 8 hours. The first 20 minutes are Saturday’s misses. Then this chapter, questions Q25–Q30, and the score of all 30. What you must know Collectors.groupingBy builds a Map . The classifier is the key. The default downstream value is a List of the elements. Collectors.counting() makes the value a Long count instead. Collectors.partitioningBy always produces both keys, true and false . IntStream.average returns OptionalDouble , because an empty stream has no average. OptionalDouble[2.0] is the print for the average of 1, 2, 3 . IntStream.range(1, 4) is 1, 2, 3 . rangeClosed(1, 4) includes 4 . mapToInt turns an object stream into an IntStream . boxed turns an IntStream back into Stream<Integer> . Map<Integer, List<String>> grouped = Stream.of(\"a\", \"bb\", \"a\") .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length)); Map<Integer, Long> counts = Stream.of(\"a\", \"bb\", \"a\") .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length, Collectors.counting())); Map<Boolean, List<String>> parts = Stream.of(\"a\", \"bb\", \"ccc\") .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(s -> s.length() > 1)); The run produces {1=[a, a], 2=[bb]} , {1=2, 2=1} , and {false=[a], true=[bb, ccc]} . Map print order can vary. The keys and the members are the result that matters. Stream.concat(Stream.of(1), Stream.of(2, 3)) is one stream of 1, 2, 3 . That is concatenation. The joined stream is parallel when either input is parallel. flatMap is decomposition: one element becomes its own stream, and those streams are joined. reduce is reduction. stream.parallel() and list.parallelStream() run the pipeline on more than one thread. sequential() switches that stream back. A parallel stream is still consumed once. forEach on a parallel stream does not promise encounter order. forEachOrdered does. A sum, or any reduce whose operator is associative and has no side effects, has the same result as the sequential stream. Do not store into an ordinary ArrayList from forEach on a parallel stream. That list is not safe for concurrent adds. Chapter 5 questions Q25. Stream.of(\"a\", \"bb\", \"a\") grouped by String::length . Which list sits under key 1 ? A [a] B [a, a] C [bb] D [a, bb] 揭曉答案 Q26. The same strings, grouped with Collectors.counting() . What is the value for key 2 ? A 1 B 2 C [bb] D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q27. Stream.of(\"a\", \"bb\", \"ccc\") partitioned by length > 1 . Which list is under false ? A [a] B [bb, ccc] C The key false is absent D [a, bb, ccc] 揭曉答案 Q28. What does IntStream.of(1, 2, 3).average() return? A 2.0 as a double B OptionalDouble[2.0] C Optional[2.0] D 2 揭曉答案 Q29. What does IntStream.range(1, 4).sum() return? A 6 B 10 C 4 D An empty optional 揭曉答案 Q30. Stream.of(\"b\", \"a\").sorted().findFirst() returns what? A \"b\" B Optional[a] C Optional.empty D An exception, because findFirst requires a present value 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card A lambda’s locals are final or effectively final. A brace body that returns a value uses return . Supplier gets, Consumer accepts, Predicate tests, Function applies. UnaryOperator is a function with one type. Primitive forms avoid boxing: IntPredicate , ToIntFunction , IntFunction . Static reference: Integer::parseInt . Bound: word::length . Unbound: String::length . Constructor: ArrayList::new . orElse always evaluates its argument. orElseGet evaluates the supplier only when the optional is empty. of(null) throws. ofNullable(null) is empty. Intermediate operations run when a terminal operation runs. A stream is consumed once. flatMap flattens a stream of streams. Stream.concat joins two streams. groupingBy builds a map of lists, or a map of counts with counting . partitioningBy always has true and false . parallel() and parallelStream() do not change an associative sum. forEach on a parallel stream has no encounter order. forEachOrdered keeps it. IntStream.average returns OptionalDouble . range excludes the end. rangeClosed includes it."}, {"href": "Week-07-glossary.html", "title": "Week 7 glossary — lambdas and streams", "hay": "Week 7 glossary — lambdas and streams Terms from Week-07-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — lambdas and method references Term Meaning Lambda A method body with no name. Its target type is a functional interface. Functional interface An interface with one abstract method. Expression lambda s -> s.length() . The value of the expression is the result. No return and no semicolon. Block lambda A body in braces. If the method returns a value, the block uses return . var parameter Allowed only inside parentheses. If one parameter uses var , every parameter does. The target type supplies the type. Effectively final A local assigned once and not assigned again. A lambda may use it. A field does not have this limit. Static method reference Integer::parseInt . The lambda argument becomes the method argument. Bound method reference word::length . The instance is already chosen. A Supplier can call it. Unbound method reference String::length or String::isEmpty . The instance arrives as the first argument. Constructor reference ArrayList::new or StringBuilder::new . The target method picks which constructor. Wednesday — the common interfaces Term Meaning Supplier<T> T get() . No arguments. Consumer<T> void accept(T) . Predicate<T> boolean test(T) . Function<T, R> R apply(T) . UnaryOperator<T> T apply(T) . A function whose input and output types match. BinaryOperator<T> T apply(T, T) . IntSupplier int getAsInt() . IntConsumer void accept(int) . IntPredicate boolean test(int) . IntFunction<R> R apply(int) . ToIntFunction<T> int applyAsInt(T) . BiFunction<T, U, R> R apply(T, U) . Runnable void run() . No result. Not in java.util.function , but the empty lambda () -> {} is one. Friday — Optional Term Meaning Optional.of Wraps a non-null value. of(null) throws NullPointerException . Optional.ofNullable Wraps a value, or returns an empty optional when the value is null . Optional.empty An optional with no value. map Applies a function when a value is present and wraps the result. On an empty optional, map stays empty and does not call the function. flatMap Like map , but the function already returns an Optional . One level is unwrapped, so you do not get Optional<Optional<T>> . orElse Always evaluates its argument, then returns the stored value if there is one. orElseGet Calls the supplier only when the optional is empty. orElseThrow() Throws NoSuchElementException when the optional is empty. Saturday and Sunday — streams Term Meaning Intermediate operation filter , map , flatMap , sorted , distinct , limit , skip , peek . Lazy. They do nothing until a terminal operation runs. Terminal operation toList , reduce , count , collect , forEach , findFirst , anyMatch , allMatch , noneMatch . It runs the pipeline. Consumed stream A stream after its terminal operation. Using it again throws IllegalStateException . Stream.toList() A list that refuses add . add throws UnsupportedOperationException . flatMap Turns each element into a stream and joins those streams. map would leave a stream of streams or a stream of lists. distinct Drops later duplicates. An ordered stream keeps the first occurrence. reduce Combines the elements. reduce(0, Integer::sum) starts from the identity 0 . anyMatch True when one element passes. allMatch True when every element passes. noneMatch True when every element fails the test. findFirst An Optional of the first element. An empty stream returns an empty optional. It does not throw. groupingBy Builds a Map . The classifier is the key. The default value is a List of the elements. counting() makes the value a Long count. partitioningBy A map with both keys, true and false , even when one side is empty. IntStream.range From the start, exclusive of the end. range(1, 4) is 1, 2, 3 . IntStream.rangeClosed Includes the end. IntStream.average Returns OptionalDouble , because an empty stream has no average. Stream.concat Joins two streams into one. The result is parallel if either input is parallel. Parallel stream parallel() or parallelStream() . A side-effect-free associative reduction matches the sequential result. forEach does not keep encounter order. forEachOrdered does. mapToInt Turns an object stream into an IntStream . boxed turns an IntStream back into Stream<Integer> ."}, {"href": "Week-08-notes.html", "title": "Week 8 notes — I/O, modules, and threads", "hay": "Week 8 notes — I/O, modules, and threads 25 hours. Weekend days are 8 hours. This file follows Week 8 of the study plan. Calendar dates are only in the 15-week plan . The gate is the mixed quiz at 65% (13 of the 20 questions in this file). When this week ends, the syllabus is closed. After that the book is a reference. Weekdays are Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, 3 hours each: 20 minutes on yesterday’s misses, 2 hours typing every listing, 40 minutes of questions and the notebook. Saturday and Sunday are 8 hours each. The extra two hours are questions. Monday’s first 20 minutes are the Week 7 misses. Day What the plan says Where it is in this file Mon Byte and character streams, Path , and Files Chapter 1, Words , the three path lines, questions Q1–Q4 Wed Serialization, locales, resource bundles Chapter 2, Bag , questions Q1–Q4 Fri Modules, compiled in a terminal Chapter 3, the two modules, the service, the broken export, questions Q1–Q4 Sat (8 h) Threads, executors, a race, virtual threads Chapter 4, Race , questions Q1–Q4 Sun (8 h) Mixed quiz and the weak-topic list The mixed set, score all 20, write the three weakest topics Same checklist as the study plan: With Path and try-with-resources, read a file, count words, and write a report. One notebook example each for resolve , relativize , and normalize . State what transient and serialVersionUID do. A resource bundle in two locales, printing a date and a currency. Search order is full locale, then language, then the base bundle. Two modules, one exports and one requires , run with java -p and -m . Then a service with provides , with , and uses . Break a missing exports once and save the error. A counter that loses updates, then the same counter fixed with synchronized or AtomicInteger . Run tasks on a fixed pool and on newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor . From memory: thread states, Runnable versus Callable , start versus run , shutdown versus shutdownNow . Calling run does not start a thread. Future.get blocks. Mixed quiz on I/O, locale, modules, and threads at 65%. Write your three weakest topics at the top of the notebook. The book is now a reference, not a reading plan. Predict each listing before you run it. Each miss goes in the notebook as one rule and a five-line program. The questions are practice in the exam’s style. They are not copied from the real exam or from a book. Try each one before its answer key. Annotations and logging are on the exam at recognition level. They sit at the end of Chapter 2. You do not build a logging application this week. Chapter 1 — Streams of bytes and characters, Path , and Files 必睇 第 14 章 I/O，pp. 812–847（ Path 、 Files 、byte 同 character streams）同 pp. 856–871（“Interacting with Users”、file attributes、走訪目錄）。 略讀 “Review of Key APIs”，p. 872。只核對筆記未出現過嘅方法名。 唔使睇 “Serializing Data”（pp. 848–855）係星期三。Review Questions 唔使做。 Monday is this chapter, Words , three notebook lines, and questions Q1–Q4. What you must know InputStream and OutputStream move bytes. Reader and Writer move characters. InputStreamReader bridges a byte stream to a character stream with a charset. BufferedReader and BufferedWriter wrap another stream so you can read lines. Close the outermost wrapper. Try-with-resources, from Week 5, is how you close it. Path is a location. Path.of(\"docs\", \"notes\", \"week.txt\") builds one. These three go in the notebook. On Windows the separator in the print is \\ . Call Result on this PC Path.of(\"docs\", \"notes\", \"week.txt\").resolve(\"extra.txt\") docs\\notes\\week.txt\\extra.txt Path.of(\"docs\", \"notes\", \"week.txt\").resolve(Path.of(\"C:\\\\temp\\\\x.txt\")) C:\\temp\\x.txt Path.of(\"docs\", \"notes\").relativize(Path.of(\"docs\", \"code\", \"A.java\")) ..\\code\\A.java Path.of(\"a\", \"..\", \"b\", \".\", \"c\").normalize() b\\c resolve of a relative path appends. resolve of an absolute path replaces the original. relativize builds the path from the first location to the second. normalize removes . and applies .. . Files.readString , Files.writeString , Files.readAllLines , Files.lines , Files.exists , Files.createDirectories , Files.copy , and Files.walk are the methods to recognize. lines and walk return streams. Close them. They throw the checked IOException . Console input and output use the same streams. System.in is an InputStream . System.out is a PrintStream , which is an OutputStream . A line from the console is new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in)).readLine() . Do not close System.in or System.out . Closing either one closes the console for the rest of the program. Words Create notes.txt in the working directory with one line, Ada loves Java . Then type this. import java.nio.file.*; public class Words { public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception { Path in = Path.of(\"notes.txt\"); String text = Files.readString(in); int words = text.isBlank() ? 0 : text.trim().split(\"\\\\s+\").length; Path report = Path.of(\"report.txt\"); Files.writeString(report, \"words=\" + words + System.lineSeparator()); System.out.println(Files.readString(report).trim()); } } The run prints: words=3 Monday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 1 questions Q1. Which type reads characters rather than raw bytes? A FileInputStream B BufferedReader C FileOutputStream D DataOutputStream 揭曉答案 Q2. base is Path.of(\"docs\", \"notes\", \"week.txt\") . What does base.resolve(Path.of(\"C:\\\\temp\\\\x.txt\")) point at, on Windows? A docs\\notes\\week.txt\\C:\\temp\\x.txt B C:\\temp\\x.txt C docs\\temp\\x.txt D The code does not compile 揭曉答案 Q3. What is Path.of(\"a\", \"..\", \"b\", \".\", \"c\").normalize() on Windows? A a\\..\\b\\.\\c B b\\c C a\\b\\c D c 揭曉答案 Q4. notes.txt contains Ada loves Java . What does Words print? A words=2 B words=3 C words=14 D An exception is thrown because writeString needs try-with-resources 揭曉答案 Chapter 2 — Serialization, locales, and bundles 必睇 第 14 章 “Serializing Data”，pp. 848–855。第 11 章 p. 646（“Formatting Numbers”，貨幣同百分比）同 pp. 651–667（locale、resource bundle、 MessageFormat ）。 唔使睇 日期 pattern（pp. 647–650）第 5 週已讀。書冇 logging 或 annotations 嘅獨立章。筆記本章尾嘅 recognition 段就係考試要認到嘅程度。 Wednesday is this chapter, Bag , and questions Q1–Q4. What you must know A class implements Serializable so ObjectOutputStream can write it and ObjectInputStream can read it back. serialVersionUID is a static final long . If the class changes shape and the stored id does not match, reading throws InvalidClassException . transient fields are not written. On the way back they receive the default for their type: 0 , false , or null . static fields are not part of one object’s saved state. Deserialization does not run the constructor. Bag import java.io.*; class Bag implements Serializable { static int constructed; int n = 1; transient int t = 2; Bag() { constructed++; } } public class BagLab { public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception { Bag bag = new Bag(); bag.n = 7; bag.t = 9; var raw = new ByteArrayOutputStream(); try (var out = new ObjectOutputStream(raw)) { out.writeObject(bag); } try (var in = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(raw.toByteArray()))) { Bag back = (Bag) in.readObject(); System.out.println(back.n + \" \" + back.t + \" \" + Bag.constructed); } } } The run prints: 7 0 1 n was saved. t came back as the default 0 . The constructor ran once, for the original new Bag() , and not again while reading. Locales and bundles A Locale is a language, and optionally a country. Locale.US and Locale.forLanguageTag(\"fr-FR\") are the two you need this week. NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance(Locale.US).format(1.5) prints $1.50 . A French locale uses a comma as the decimal mark and the euro sign. NumberFormat.getPercentInstance(Locale.US).format(0.25) prints 25% , because the percent formatter multiplies by 100. DateTimeFormatter.ofLocalizedDate(FormatStyle.SHORT).withLocale(Locale.US) prints 12/21/26 for 21 December 2026. ResourceBundle.getBundle(\"messages\", Locale.FRANCE).getString(\"hello\") is the call that walks the search list below. MessageFormat.format(\"Hello {0}\", \"Ada\") replaces {0} and returns Hello Ada . {1} is the second argument. MessageFormat.format(\"Hello {1}\", \"Ada\") has no argument at index 1, so the result stays Hello {1} . Write one argument per index you use. A resource bundle is a set of property files. For Locale.FRANCE and the base name messages , the search order on the checklist is: messages_fr_FR messages_fr messages If the key is still missing, Java repeats that idea for the default locale. If the base bundle is missing too, getBundle throws MissingResourceException . A key found in a parent file is used when the more specific file does not define it. Put messages.properties ( hello=Hello ) and messages_fr.properties ( hello=Bonjour ) on the classpath and print both locales. That is the two-locale checklist item. Recognition only @Override checks an override. @FunctionalInterface checks that an interface has one abstract method. @Deprecated marks an element you should stop using. @SuppressWarnings silences a named warning. You will see these in snippets. You do not need to declare your own annotation type. System.Logger and java.util.logging are the logging names on the exam. A logger has levels. You log a level and a message. You do not configure a logging framework this week. Wednesday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 2 questions Q1. After the round trip in BagLab , what are n , t , and constructed ? A 7 9 1 B 7 0 1 C 7 0 2 D 1 2 1 揭曉答案 Q2. What does transient do? A It saves the field first B It skips the field, and the restored field gets the type’s default C It makes the field static D It encrypts the field 揭曉答案 Q3. What does NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance(Locale.US).format(1.5) print? A 1.50 B $1.50 C 1,50 € D USD 1.5 揭曉答案 Q4. messages_fr.properties defines hello , and messages.properties defines hello and bye . For Locale.FRENCH , which file supplies bye ? A messages_fr.properties only, so the key is missing B messages.properties C messages_fr_FR.properties D The search starts at the base file 揭曉答案 Chapter 3 — Modules 必睇 第 12 章 Modules，pp. 684–718（由介紹到 jlink ）同 pp. 724–734（module 種類同 migration）。 唔使睇 jpackage （p. 719）。Review Questions 唔使做。 Friday is this chapter. Compile it in a terminal. Questions Q1–Q4 come after the broken export. What you must know A module is a named group of packages, described by module-info.java in the module root. requires other reads that module. exports pkg makes that package visible to modules that read this one. provides com.example.api.Speaker with com.example.hello.HelloSpeaker offers a service. uses com.example.api.Speaker means this module loads that service. A package that is not exported is not visible, even when the other module requires you. Create this tree under a folder named mods . mods/com.example.api/module-info.java module com.example.api { exports com.example.api; } mods/com.example.api/com/example/api/Speaker.java package com.example.api; public interface Speaker { String speak(); } mods/com.example.hello/module-info.java module com.example.hello { requires com.example.api; provides com.example.api.Speaker with com.example.hello.HelloSpeaker; } mods/com.example.hello/com/example/hello/HelloSpeaker.java package com.example.hello; import com.example.api.Speaker; public class HelloSpeaker implements Speaker { public String speak() { return \"hello\"; } } mods/com.example.app/module-info.java module com.example.app { requires com.example.api; uses com.example.api.Speaker; } mods/com.example.app/com/example/app/Main.java package com.example.app; import com.example.api.Speaker; import java.util.ServiceLoader; public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { ServiceLoader.load(Speaker.class).forEach(s -> System.out.println(s.speak())); } } From the folder that contains mods : javac -d out --module-source-path mods mods\\com.example.api\\module-info.java mods\\com.example.api\\com\\example\\api\\Speaker.java mods\\com.example.hello\\module-info.java mods\\com.example.hello\\com\\example\\hello\\HelloSpeaker.java mods\\com.example.app\\module-info.java mods\\com.example.app\\com\\example\\app\\Main.java java -p out -m com.example.app/com.example.app.Main The run prints: hello -p is the module path. -m is the module and the main class. Break it once. Remove exports com.example.api; and compile an app that imports Speaker . Save this error in the notebook: package com.example.api is not visible (package com.example.api is declared in module com.example.api, which does not export it) The hidden-package drill in the checklist uses the same message. Put the package you actually hid into that sentence. Migration, jars, and a runtime image exports makes the public types of a package usable by other modules. opens allows reflective access, including setAccessible on members that are not public. exports alone does not grant that reflection. exports com.example.hello to com.example.app and opens com.example.hello to com.example.app limit the right to one named module. requires transitive com.example.util means a module that requires yours also reads com.example.util . That caller can use com.example.util ’s exported types without writing its own requires . A plain requires does not pass the dependency on. A JAR with no module-info that you put on the classpath belongs to the unnamed module. The unnamed module reads every resolved module, and all of its packages are open. A named module cannot requires the unnamed module. The same JAR placed on the module path is an automatic module. Its name comes from the JAR file name, unless the manifest sets Automatic-Module-Name . An automatic module exports and opens every package, and it reads every other resolved module, including the unnamed module. jar --create --file app.jar -C out . builds a non-modular jar when out has no module-info.class . Run it with java -cp app.jar com.example.app.Main . A jar that contains module-info.class is a modular jar. Run it with java -p app.jar -m com.example.app/com.example.app.Main . jlink builds a runtime image with only the modules you name and the modules they need. The module path includes the JDK’s jmods folder, because that is where java.base lives. jlink --module-path \"%JAVA_HOME%\\jmods;out\" --add-modules com.example.app --output image The program is then image\\bin\\java -m com.example.app/com.example.app.Main . Friday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 3 questions Q1. Which directive lets another module read a package? A requires B exports C uses D opens is required for every read 揭曉答案 Q2. The app module calls ServiceLoader.load(Speaker.class) . Which pair belongs in the module descriptors? A The provider uses the service, and the app provides it B The provider provides ... with ... , and the app uses the service C Both modules exports the service class D requires alone publishes the service 揭曉答案 Q3. What does java -p out -m com.example.app/com.example.app.Main do? A It compiles the module B It runs Main using the modules in out C It exports every package D It ignores module-info.java 揭曉答案 Q4. A module requires another module, and the package it imports is not exported. What is the compile error about? A A missing main B A cycle of requires C The package is not visible, because the module does not export it D ServiceLoader cannot see a class 揭曉答案 Chapter 4 — Threads 必睇 第 13 章 Concurrency，pp. 746–785：threads、virtual threads、executors、 Lock 、 CyclicBarrier 、concurrent collections、race。 唔使睇 “Working with Parallel Streams”（pp. 786–794）第 7 週讀過就跳過。Review Questions 唔使做。 Saturday is 8 hours: this chapter, Race , and questions Q1–Q4. What you must know A thread’s states are NEW , RUNNABLE , BLOCKED , WAITING , TIMED_WAITING , and TERMINATED . BLOCKED means it is waiting to enter a synchronized block. WAITING is a wait with no timeout, such as join() or Object.wait() . TIMED_WAITING is sleep , wait(millis) , or join(millis) . Runnable.run returns void and cannot throw a checked exception. Callable.call returns a value and can throw Exception . thread.start() schedules the thread. thread.run() , or calling run on the Runnable yourself, executes the body on the current thread and does not schedule a new one. Calling start twice throws IllegalThreadStateException . ExecutorService runs tasks. Executors.newFixedThreadPool(1) reuses one platform thread. Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor() starts a virtual thread per task. Future.get blocks until the task finishes and returns its value. shutdown rejects new tasks and lets the current tasks finish. shutdownNow interrupts running tasks and returns the tasks that had not started. A plain int updated from two threads can lose increments. The two reads can see the same number and both write that number plus one. AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet and a synchronized block do not lose those updates. synchronized releases the monitor when the block ends, including when the block throws. A ReentrantLock does not. Call lock() and put unlock() in a finally block. The same thread may lock it again. Each lock needs its own unlock . ConcurrentHashMap is a map that concurrent tasks may update. It rejects a null key and a null value. HashMap accepts one null key and does not survive concurrent updates. list.parallelStream() is the parallel stream from Week 7. A sum of the elements matches the sequential sum. forEach may run in any order. Thread.ofVirtual().start(...) and Thread.startVirtualThread(...) create virtual threads. Do not build a pool of virtual threads by hand. The per-task executor is the tool from the checklist. Race The atomic counter is the one to trust. Two virtual threads each increment 1000 times. import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger; public class Race { public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception { AtomicInteger atom = new AtomicInteger(); Thread a = Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) atom.incrementAndGet(); }); Thread b = Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) atom.incrementAndGet(); }); a.join(); b.join(); System.out.println(atom.get()); } } The run prints: 2000 For the broken counter, repeat the same loops on a shared int with count = count + 1 and no lock. Run it several times. Some totals are below 2000. That is the lost update. Then wrap the increment in synchronized (lock) and the total is 2000 again. A fixed pool of one thread, given Callable<String> that returns \"ok\" , prints ok from future.get() , and shutdown() lets that task finish. Saturday’s questions are Q1–Q4. Chapter 4 questions Q1. What does calling run() on a Runnable do? A It schedules a new thread B It runs the body on the current thread C It moves a thread from NEW to TERMINATED without running the body D It throws IllegalThreadStateException 揭曉答案 Q2. What does Future.get do when the task is still running? A It returns null immediately B It blocks until the task finishes C It cancels the task D It throws IllegalStateException immediately 揭曉答案 Q3. Which pair matches the methods? A shutdown interrupts running tasks. shutdownNow only rejects new ones B shutdown rejects new tasks and lets current tasks finish. shutdownNow interrupts running tasks C Both wait forever for new tasks D Both are illegal on a virtual-thread executor 揭曉答案 Q4. Two threads each run count = count + 1 a thousand times on a shared int , with no lock. Which statement is true? A The total is always 2000 B The total can be below 2000 C The code does not compile D The total is always 1000 揭曉答案 Sunday — mixed quiz, then the syllabus closes 唔使睇 混合測驗用筆記計分。由今日起書變成參考，唔使再逐章重讀。 The first 20 minutes are Saturday’s misses. Then this set. Score Chapter 1 (4), Chapter 2 (4), Chapter 3 (4), Chapter 4 (4), and the mixed set (4). 13 or more of the 20 questions is the gate. Write your three weakest topics at the top of the notebook. The book is now a reference. Week 9 starts from that list. Mixed set M1. Path.of(\"docs\", \"notes\").relativize(Path.of(\"docs\", \"code\", \"A.java\")) on Windows is which path? A docs\\code\\A.java B ..\\code\\A.java C notes\\code\\A.java D code\\A.java 揭曉答案 M2. What is serialVersionUID for? A It saves transient fields B It names the class shape. A mismatch with the stream throws InvalidClassException C It locks the object during readObject D It replaces Serializable 揭曉答案 M3. provides and uses are in place, and java -p out -m com.example.app/com.example.app.Main runs. What does the service Main in this chapter print? A nothing B hello C the module name D An exception, because the app did not require the provider module 揭曉答案 M4. Which state means a thread is in Thread.sleep ? A NEW B BLOCKED C TIMED_WAITING D TERMINATED 揭曉答案 Rules to copy onto one card Byte streams use InputStream and OutputStream . Character streams use Reader and Writer . Close the outer wrapper. Do not close System.in or System.out . resolve appends a relative path and replaces with an absolute path. relativize is the path from one location to another. normalize drops . and applies .. . transient is not saved and comes back as the default. Deserialization does not run the constructor. serialVersionUID names the class shape. Bundle search for a locale is the full locale, then the language, then the base file. Currency and short dates follow the locale. Percent format multiplies by 100. MessageFormat replaces {0} . exports makes a package visible. opens allows reflection. requires transitive passes a read on. provides ... with ... and uses publish and load a service. A missing exports makes the package not visible. Classpath code is the unnamed module. A JAR with no module-info on the module path is an automatic module. jlink builds a runtime image. start schedules a thread. run does not. Future.get blocks. shutdown finishes current work. shutdownNow interrupts it. A shared int increment can lose updates. AtomicInteger or synchronized keeps them. ReentrantLock needs unlock in finally . ConcurrentHashMap rejects null. Virtual threads are one per task, not a hand-built pool."}, {"href": "Week-08-glossary.html", "title": "Week 8 glossary — I/O, modules, and threads", "hay": "Week 8 glossary — I/O, modules, and threads Terms from Week-08-notes.md . The exam words are English. Monday — files and paths Term Meaning InputStream / OutputStream Move bytes. Reader / Writer Move characters. InputStreamReader bridges a byte stream to a character stream. BufferedReader Wraps another reader so you can read lines. Close the outermost wrapper. Path A location. Path.of builds one. resolve A relative argument is appended. An absolute argument replaces the original path. relativize The path from the first location to the second. normalize Removes . and applies .. . Files.readString Reads a whole file as one String . Throws the checked IOException . Files.writeString Writes a String to a file. Throws IOException . Files.lines / Files.walk Return streams. Close them, usually with try-with-resources. System.in / System.out Console byte streams. System.in is an InputStream . System.out is a PrintStream . Do not close either one. Wednesday — serialization, locales, recognition Term Meaning Serializable The marker interface that lets ObjectOutputStream write an object and ObjectInputStream read it back. serialVersionUID A static final long that names the class shape. A mismatch with the stream throws InvalidClassException . transient The field is not written. On the way back it receives the default: 0 , false , or null . Deserialization Restores the object without running its constructor. static fields are not part of one object’s saved state. Locale A language, and optionally a country. Locale.US . Locale.forLanguageTag(\"fr-FR\") . NumberFormat Formats a number for a locale. The US currency format of 1.5 is $1.50 . getPercentInstance multiplies by 100, so 0.25 in Locale.US is 25% . Resource bundle A set of property files for one base name. ResourceBundle.getBundle(\"messages\", locale).getString(\"hello\") . For a French locale the search is messages_fr_FR , then messages_fr , then messages . A missing key can come from the parent file. MessageFormat Replaces {0} , {1} , and so on. MessageFormat.format(\"Hello {0}\", \"Ada\") is Hello Ada . MissingResourceException Thrown when getBundle cannot find the base bundle. @Override Checks that a method really overrides. @FunctionalInterface Checks that an interface has one abstract method. @Deprecated Marks an element you should stop using. @SuppressWarnings Silences a named warning. Logger System.Logger or java.util.logging . A call records a level and a message. Recognition level only this week. Friday — modules Term Meaning Module A named group of packages, described by module-info.java . requires This module reads another module. exports Makes a package visible to modules that read this one. A package that is not exported is not visible. provides ... with ... This module offers a service implementation. uses This module loads that service with ServiceLoader . The consumer does not require the provider by name. Module path The directories or jars given to -p . -m The module and the main class, as in com.example.app/com.example.app.Main . Not visible The compiler error when code imports a package that its required module does not export. opens Allows reflective access to a package. exports does not grant setAccessible . requires transitive A module that reads yours also reads that dependency. Unnamed module All code on the classpath. It reads every resolved module. A named module cannot requires it. Automatic module A JAR with no module-info on the module path. It exports and opens every package. The name comes from the JAR file name, or from Automatic-Module-Name in the manifest. Modular jar A jar that contains module-info.class . Run it with -p and -m . jlink Builds a runtime image that contains the modules you name and the modules they need. Saturday — threads Term Meaning NEW A Thread that has not been started. RUNNABLE Eligible to run, or running. BLOCKED Waiting to enter a synchronized block. WAITING Waiting with no timeout, such as join() or Object.wait() . TIMED_WAITING sleep , wait(millis) , or join(millis) . TERMINATED The body has finished. Runnable void run() . Cannot throw a checked exception. Callable call returns a value and can throw Exception . start Schedules the thread. Calling it twice throws IllegalThreadStateException . run An ordinary call. The body runs on the current thread. It does not schedule a new thread. ExecutorService Runs tasks. newFixedThreadPool reuses platform threads. newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor starts a virtual thread per task. Future.get Blocks until the task finishes and returns its value. A task that throws is delivered as ExecutionException . shutdown Rejects new tasks and lets current tasks finish. shutdownNow Interrupts running tasks and returns the tasks that had not started. Lost update Two threads read the same int and both write that number plus one. One increment disappears. AtomicInteger incrementAndGet updates the number without a lost update. A synchronized block can do the same. Virtual thread A lightweight thread. Start one with Thread.ofVirtual().start or Thread.startVirtualThread . Do not build a pool of them by hand. ReentrantLock lock() , then unlock() in a finally block. Unlike synchronized , leaving the block does not unlock it. The same thread may lock it again, and each lock needs an unlock . ConcurrentHashMap A map that concurrent tasks may update. A null key or a null value throws NullPointerException . parallelStream A parallel stream of a collection. An associative sum matches the sequential sum. forEach may run in any order."}];
